#!/bin/sh
# install-library — deploy-pin the Library (platform/decisions/0059).
#
# WHY. com.convivy.library's ProgramArguments used to exec
# ~/Convivy/library/serve.sh directly — the development
# checkout, and its WorkingDirectory too. Whatever was checked out there was
# what served, including an unmerged branch, and launchd re-reads that on
# every restart, including an unattended crash-restart under KeepAlive. On
# 2026-08-20 the service served code 19 hours stale because the checkout sat
# on a branch nobody switched back after merging through `gh`.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES, per ADR-0059 rules 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8:
#   1. Reads a git REF (default main), never the live working tree, via
#      `git archive` — a `cp`/`rsync` of the checkout would ignore
#      .gitignore and sweep the Library's gitignored credential files into
#      the deploy area (rule 7).
#   2. Extracts a COPY of the whole tracked tree at that commit into
#      ~/.local/deploy/library/ (rule 2: copies, not symlinks). Nothing
#      outside git's tracked blobs reaches the deploy area, which is what
#      makes this safe where `cp -r`/`rsync` would not be.
#   3. Builds a fresh venv inside that same deploy area from the exported
#      requirements.txt, so the interpreter travels with the code rather
#      than depending on the checkout's own .venv (rule 4).
#   4. Runs the deployed serve.sh's own `--check` against the staged build,
#      BEFORE the swap — the same interpreter-trust guard that has always
#      gated a start now also gates an install, so a broken pip install or
#      an untrusted interpreter chain never reaches the swap step, and it
#      is the one check for this, not a second copy of it (the task's
#      "that check moves with the service rather than being replaced").
#      `--check` also imports `app` itself, under the plist this install is
#      about to make live (`LIBRARY_CHECK_PLIST`, below) — a job plist
#      missing a variable `app.py` or its imports require at import time now
#      fails THIS step rather than crash-looping the live service (library
#      issue issue_01M3W62VVYJPQ6N9Y85DF3ZQYP).
#   5. Copies (never symlinks) the plist template out of the repo into
#      ~/Library/LaunchAgents/, refusing to clobber a live plist that has
#      drifted from what THIS installer itself last put there — its record
#      at ~/.local/deploy/library-plist.sha256, not the new template — unless
#      --force is given (rule 8). A template change over an unedited live
#      plist is therefore never refused as drift; only an operator's own
#      hand edit is. This comparison happens FIRST, before step 2, not
#      after: see rule 6 below.
#   6. When that comparison says the live plist is about to change (and the
#      change is not refused), bootout's the job BEFORE step 2 ever stages
#      anything — so neither the old code under the new environment nor the
#      new code under the old environment is ever live — then, once steps 2-5
#      land the new code and plist together, bootstraps it back and verifies,
#      with bounded retries, that it is running, that every variable the new
#      plist sets is the variable `launchctl print` reports back, and that
#      the EFFECTIVE `exit timeout` `launchctl print` reports exceeds the
#      deployed app.py's own drain deadline (issue_01M3N7NB9218HXKQDPD3NF1BJH:
#      the plist's own `ExitTimeOut` integer is not what launchd necessarily
#      applies). Skipped entirely whenever step 5's comparison found no
#      change, or the
#      change was refused as drift.
#
# Never touches ~/.library — the Chart House and the Archives (the vaults
# LIBRARY_DB and LIBRARY_CLIENT_TOKENS point at) already live outside any
# repo, read by absolute path, and this script has no line that opens,
# writes, or copies anything under that directory (rule 3). Confirm by
# reading this file: the only paths it writes are under $DEPLOY_DIR and
# $LAUNCH_AGENTS.
#
# Idempotent and re-run-safe: each run stages a full new deploy area and
# swaps it in atomically (old directory renamed aside, new one moved into
# place) — never a partial mix of two commits' files, and a staged build that
# fails its own --check is discarded before the swap ever runs, leaving
# whatever was already deployed untouched. The same guarantee now covers the
# job itself: when this install is reloading the job, the renamed-aside old
# release is kept, not deleted, until the reload verifies — see the
# "RELOADING INSTALL, STEP BY STEP" table below for exactly what each step
# leaves on disk and loaded if it's the one that fails, and what recovery
# does about it.
#
# Does not touch the vault write cutover's environment (LIBRARY_DB /
# LIBRARY_WRITES_ENABLED) itself — those live in the plist this script only
# copies, never edits.
#
# DOES reload the launchd job (bootout, then stage+swap+plist-write, then
# bootstrap+verify) when the plist it is about to install differs from the
# one that is live. See the step table below for why bootout has to come
# before the code is staged rather than after.
#
# ALSO fast-forwards the checkout and migrates the vault before any of the
# above, on an ordinary run: when $REPO has the named branch (main, by
# default) checked out, with a clean tree and no merge or rebase in
# progress, and that branch is behind origin, this runs `git merge
# --ff-only` to catch it up; when the vault's alembic revision is then
# behind the tree's new head, this backs it up with `sqlite3 .backup` and
# runs `alembic upgrade head` from $REPO before staging anything. Neither
# step ever stashes, resets, checks out, or cleans on the operator's
# behalf, and neither runs when the branch named isn't the one checked
# out — only an operator's own `git pull`/`alembic upgrade` reaches that
# case, same as always. `--no-pull` and `--no-migrate` each turn one step
# off, back to today's refuse-and-say-so behaviour; a pending revision
# that sets `LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY = False` (see alembic/README) always stops
# instead of migrating, flag or no flag.
set -eu

# --- shared parsers, defined up front so the hidden test entry point right
# below can call them without running the rest of this script. Neither is
# part of the public interface (usage() does not list --internal-*);
# tests/test_install_library.py invokes --internal-launchctl-env-pairs
# directly, on stdin, against a captured real `launchctl print` fixture, so
# the parser under test is the exact one this script runs, not a copy of it.

# The token every shipped launchd/*.plist carries in place of a literal home
# directory — launchd does not expand `$HOME` or `~` in a plist it loads, so
# the checked-in template can never name the installing user's home as a
# literal path and still be the same file on every machine. Rendered out by
# _render_plist below, always against the real $HOME this script is running
# with, never a path hardcoded to one operating system's own convention —
# the Library has to run on Linux too (company/decisions/0010).
PLIST_HOME_PLACEHOLDER='@@LIBRARY_HOME@@'

# Replaces every literal occurrence of $1 in the text on stdin with $2,
# treating both as plain strings — no regex, and no special meaning for `&`
# or `\` in either, which is what sed's own `s/.../.../` or awk's own
# gsub(regex, repl) replacement argument would both give those two
# characters. $2 here is always $HOME, outside this script's control, so a
# substitution mechanism that treated it as a pattern or a replacement
# directive could corrupt the render on a $HOME containing either character.
# Both strings reach awk through ENVIRON, which awk reads raw: `awk -v`
# processes escape sequences in its value, so a `\t` in $HOME would render
# as a tab.
_replace_literal() {
  _RL_FIND="$1" _RL_REPL="$2" awk '
    BEGIN { find = ENVIRON["_RL_FIND"]; repl = ENVIRON["_RL_REPL"] }
    {
      line = $0
      out = ""
      flen = length(find)
      while ((idx = index(line, find)) > 0) {
        out = out substr(line, 1, idx - 1) repl
        line = substr(line, idx + flen)
      }
      print out line
    }
  '
}

# Renders plist template $1 (read from the filesystem) against $HOME,
# printing the result to stdout. The one substitution a shipped plist ever
# needs: $PLIST_HOME_PLACEHOLDER for the installing user's own $HOME.
_render_plist() {
  _replace_literal "$PLIST_HOME_PLACEHOLDER" "$HOME" < "$1"
}

# Parses a plist file's own EnvironmentVariables into KEY=VALUE lines.
_plist_env_pairs() {
  awk '
    /<key>EnvironmentVariables<\/key>/ { in_env=1; next }
    in_env && /<\/dict>/ { in_env=0 }
    in_env && /<key>/ {
      k=$0; sub(/.*<key>/,"",k); sub(/<\/key>.*/,"",k); pending=k; next
    }
    in_env && pending != "" && /<string>/ {
      v=$0; sub(/.*<string>/,"",v); sub(/<\/string>.*/,"",v)
      print pending "=" v
      pending=""
    }
  ' "$1"
}

# Reads `app.py`'s own `_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS` out of the deployed source
# file at $1, truncated to whole seconds — `launchctl print`'s own `exit
# timeout` is always an integer, and the constant carries a `.0` a plain
# shell `-le` cannot compare against. Grep, not `import app`: importing the
# real module pulls in every dependency app.py imports (fastapi, store, the
# rest of the service) for a value that never leaves module scope, and this
# runs from this script's own interpreter, not the staged venv.
#
# Matches only a top-level line — one that starts in column 0 with the exact
# name — so a decoy in a comment, an indented copy, a same-prefix name
# (`_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS_OLD`), or a string elsewhere in app.py can never
# supply the value. Accepts both the plain assignment
# (`_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS = 50.0`) and the annotated form
# (`_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS: float = 50.0`), and strips a trailing `#`
# comment before parsing the number. Prints nothing — never a non-numeric
# string — when the line isn't found or what follows `=` isn't a plain
# integer once whitespace, the annotation and any trailing comment are
# stripped: this fail-closed shape is what lets the caller treat an empty
# result as "could not read", the same as an unreadable `exit timeout`
# (issue_01M3N7NB9218HXKQDPD3NF1BJH round 1: a skipped comparison here used
# to verify a reload silently instead of failing it).
_drain_deadline_seconds() {
  awk '
    /^_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS[ \t]*(:[ \t]*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[ \t]*)?=/ {
      line = $0
      sub(/^[^=]*=/, "", line)
      sub(/#.*/, "", line)
      gsub(/[ \t]/, "", line)
      sub(/\.[0-9]*$/, "", line)
      if (line ~ /^[0-9]+$/) print line
      exit
    }
  ' "$1" 2>/dev/null
}

# Reads `launchctl print`'s text from stdin and emits ONLY its own
# `environment = { ... }` block as KEY=VALUE lines — never `inherited
# environment = { ... }` or `default environment = { ... }`, which real
# `launchctl print` output also emits, each perfectly capable of containing
# the literal substring "environment = {" itself (round 3 review, PR #157:
# an unanchored `/environment[ \t]*=[ \t]*\{/` matches all three block
# openers, pooling their pairs together — PATH, for one, is set in all
# three, to three different values). The match requires "environment" to be
# the FIRST non-whitespace token on the line, which the plist's own comment
# (`launchd/com.convivy.library.plist`, over EnvironmentVariables) already
# documents as the anchored form that tells this file apart from the two
# it must not read: neither "inherited" nor "default" starts a line this
# way. tests/fixtures/launchctl_print_library.txt is a real, once-captured
# `launchctl print` of this job, checked for secrets before it was
# committed (the environment it holds is paths and flags only) — the parser
# is tested against it directly via --internal-launchctl-env-pairs below.
_launchctl_env_actual_pairs() {
  awk '
    /^[ \t]*environment[ \t]*=[ \t]*\{[ \t]*$/ { in_env=1; next }
    in_env {
      line=$0
      gsub(/^[ \t]+|[ \t]+$/, "", line)
      if (line == "}") { in_env=0; next }
      idx = index(line, " => ")
      if (idx > 0) {
        print substr(line, 1, idx-1) "=" substr(line, idx+4)
      }
    }
  '
}

if [ "${1:-}" = "--internal-launchctl-env-pairs" ]; then
  _launchctl_env_actual_pairs
  exit 0
fi

# Not part of the public interface either (usage() does not list it) — lets
# an operator render one of the four plists this script never installs
# itself (cold-tier, cert-expiry-check, ci-result-fetch, deploy-watch — each
# one's header points here) before copying it to ~/Library/LaunchAgents by
# hand, and lets tests exercise the real renderer directly rather than a
# reimplementation of it. From the deploy area, for one of the first three
# (deploy-pinned, so the deploy area's own copy is the one that matters):
#
#   bin/install-library --internal-render-plist \
#     launchd/com.convivy.library.cold-tier.plist \
#     > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.convivy.library.cold-tier.plist
#
# deploy-watch is CHECKOUT-pinned instead (its own header says why), so
# render it from the checkout rather than the deploy area:
#
#   bin/install-library --internal-render-plist \
#     launchd/com.convivy.library.deploy-watch.plist \
#     > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.convivy.library.deploy-watch.plist
#
# Copying the template raw installs the literal placeholder, which launchd
# does not expand.
if [ "${1:-}" = "--internal-render-plist" ]; then
  [ -n "${2:-}" ] || { echo "install-library: --internal-render-plist needs a path" >&2; exit 2; }
  _render_plist "$2"
  exit 0
fi

usage() {
  cat >&2 <<'EOF'
usage: install-library [--ref=<git-ref>] [--repo=<path>]
                        [--allow-non-main] [--force] [--status]
                        [--allow-schema-behind] [--no-pull] [--no-migrate]
                        [--python=<path>]

  --ref=<git-ref>     Ref to install from (default: main). Classified by what
                       it RESOLVES TO, not how it's spelled: when that is a
                       local branch (a bare name, HEAD, or any refs/heads/...
                       spelling) that $REPO does not currently have checked
                       out, fetches origin first and refuses to install if
                       the branch differs from origin's, in either direction
                       — unchanged from before. When it IS the branch $REPO
                       has checked out (true of the default, main, on an
                       ordinary deploy checkout), fetches origin first and,
                       if behind, fast-forwards it with `git merge
                       --ff-only` instead of refusing (see --no-pull); still
                       refuses, exactly as before, if ahead of or diverged
                       from origin. When that is an origin remote-tracking
                       ref (origin/<branch> or refs/remotes/origin/<branch>),
                       fetches origin first and installs whatever that
                       resolves to, so it's never stale. Either way, with no
                       "origin" remote configured to verify against, this
                       refuses rather than installing unverified. A full sha
                       or a tag deploys as given, unfetched, with no origin
                       needed.
  --repo=<path>       Checkout to read the ref from (default: this script's
                       own repo — resolved from argv[0], not the caller's cwd).
  --allow-non-main    Required to install anything other than main (rule 6:
                       the deploy area is installed from main).
  --force             Overwrite a live plist that has drifted from what this
                       installer last put there.
  --status             Print what commit is currently deployed, the recorded
                       plist sha256, and whether the live plist matches it,
                       plus the vault's alembic revision, the tree's
                       migration head, whether they match, whether $REPO's
                       local main is behind origin/main, and (when the vault
                       is behind) the pending revisions; exit. Never changes
                       anything, and never fast-forwards or migrates.
  --allow-schema-behind
                       Required to install when the vault's alembic revision
                       is unknown to the tree or could not be read at all —
                       checked against the LIBRARY_DB the CURRENTLY LIVE
                       plist sets (library issue
                       issue_01M1JKC0AC3ZBBA3CXC2DGSVYH: the running service
                       can self-restart onto newly staged code within
                       seconds, with no migration ever applied). Also skips
                       the auto-migrate below when the vault is simply
                       behind, installing with the old schema state instead
                       of migrating it. Skipped entirely when nothing is
                       live yet to protect (no plist at $PLIST_LIVE).
  --no-pull           Never fast-forward $REPO's checked-out branch — refuse
                       instead whenever it differs from origin, in either
                       direction, same as before this installer could pull
                       at all.
  --no-migrate        Never apply a pending migration — refuse instead
                       whenever the vault's alembic revision is behind the
                       tree's, same as before this installer could migrate
                       at all.
  --python=<path>     Interpreter to build the staged venv with, instead of
                       the hardcoded macOS/Homebrew search below. Selects
                       only which interpreter BUILDS the venv — the staged
                       build still has to pass serve.sh's own ownership walk
                       (rule 4) before it can go live, unchanged by this.
EOF
  exit 2
}

REPO="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
REF="main"
ALLOW_NON_MAIN=0
PLIST_FORCE=0
STATUS_ONLY=0
ALLOW_SCHEMA_BEHIND=0
NO_PULL=0
NO_MIGRATE=0
PYTHON_OVERRIDE=""

for arg in "$@"; do
  case "$arg" in
    --ref=*) REF="${arg#--ref=}" ;;
    --repo=*) REPO="${arg#--repo=}" ;;
    --allow-non-main) ALLOW_NON_MAIN=1 ;;
    --force) PLIST_FORCE=1 ;;
    --status) STATUS_ONLY=1 ;;
    --allow-schema-behind) ALLOW_SCHEMA_BEHIND=1 ;;
    --no-pull) NO_PULL=1 ;;
    --no-migrate) NO_MIGRATE=1 ;;
    --python=*) PYTHON_OVERRIDE="${arg#--python=}" ;;
    -h|--help) usage ;;
    *) echo "install-library: unknown option: $arg" >&2; usage ;;
  esac
done

DEPLOY_DIR="$HOME/.local/deploy/library"
# INSTALL_LIBRARY_LAUNCH_AGENTS, INSTALL_LIBRARY_LAUNCHCTL (below) and
# INSTALL_LIBRARY_LAUNCHD_DOMAIN (below, once $LABEL is known) let a test
# harness redirect every launchd-facing operation this script makes — the
# LaunchAgents directory it writes a plist into, the launchctl binary it
# invokes, and the domain it targets — at a scratch directory, a stub binary
# and a throwaway domain instead of the real ones, without this script
# needing to know it is under test. Each defaults to the real value, so an
# operator run is unchanged.
LAUNCH_AGENTS="${INSTALL_LIBRARY_LAUNCH_AGENTS:-$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents}"
PLIST_NAME="com.convivy.library.plist"
PLIST_LIVE="$LAUNCH_AGENTS/$PLIST_NAME"
PLIST_RECORD="$HOME/.local/deploy/library-plist.sha256"
LAUNCHCTL="${INSTALL_LIBRARY_LAUNCHCTL:-launchctl}"

say() { printf '\033[1;34m▸\033[0m %s\n' "$*"; }
warn() { printf '\033[1;33m!\033[0m %s\n' "$*" >&2; }

# Find a real sha256 tool, never one a poisoned PATH could hand back — the
# same principle, and the same shape, as the PYTHON_BIN search below: fixed
# absolute paths first, `command -v` only as a last resort, fail closed if
# neither turns up anything. Runs here, ahead of both the --status branch and
# the drift decision, since --status hashes the live plist too and used to
# reach that entirely via `command -v shasum`. shasum is the macOS tool,
# sha256sum the Linux one (serve.sh's own UNAME_S guard is the same split).
HASH_TOOL=""
for cand in \
    /usr/bin/shasum \
    /opt/homebrew/bin/shasum \
    /usr/local/bin/shasum \
    /usr/bin/sha256sum \
    /bin/sha256sum \
    /opt/homebrew/bin/sha256sum \
    /usr/local/bin/sha256sum
do
  if [ -x "$cand" ]; then HASH_TOOL="$cand"; break; fi
done
if [ -z "$HASH_TOOL" ]; then
  HASH_TOOL="$(command -v shasum 2>/dev/null || command -v sha256sum 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi
[ -n "$HASH_TOOL" ] || {
  echo "install-library: no sha256 tool found (checked /usr/bin/shasum," >&2
  echo "  /opt/homebrew/bin, /usr/local/bin, /usr/bin/sha256sum, /bin, and PATH)" >&2
  exit 1
}

# Hashes $1 with sha256, printing just the digest, via $HASH_TOOL above.
_sha256() {
  case "$HASH_TOOL" in
    */shasum) "$HASH_TOOL" -a 256 "$1" | awk '{print $1}' ;;
    *) "$HASH_TOOL" "$1" | awk '{print $1}' ;;
  esac
}

# Records $1's sha256 as what this installer itself last put at $PLIST_LIVE
# — the record the drift decision below reads, never the new template.
# Written atomically (temp file, then `mv` within the same directory) so a
# reader never observes a partial write. Tolerates its own failure (warns
# and returns 0 either way) rather than aborting an install — under `set -e`
# a bare failure here would abort a reload this function runs AFTER
# verifying succeeded, over a bookkeeping file that isn't itself load-bearing
# for anything already running.
#
# This record guards against an OPERATOR'S OWN hand edit going unnoticed, not
# against a same-user adversary. The direct routes to the same plist swap —
# editing $PLIST_LIVE, writing this file, running `install-library --force`
# — are all overt, timed by whoever runs them. A same-user process can also
# forge this file to match a live plist the operator has hand-edited, which
# makes the OPERATOR'S NEXT ROUTINE install (no --force) discard that edit
# silently, with no warning and a clean --status afterward — deferred and
# invisible where the direct routes are not. That gap is accepted: anything
# running as this user can already overwrite $PLIST_LIVE and reload the job,
# so the forgery grants no privilege beyond what a same-UID process already
# has, and closing it needs the deploy state to live under a user the agents
# don't run as, which is not built.
_write_plist_record() {
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$PLIST_RECORD")" 2>/dev/null || true
  tmp="$PLIST_RECORD.tmp.$$"
  if _sha256 "$1" > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null && mv "$tmp" "$PLIST_RECORD" 2>/dev/null; then
    :
  else
    rm -f "$tmp" 2>/dev/null || true
    warn "could not record the installed plist's sha256 at $PLIST_RECORD — a later install may need --force unnecessarily"
  fi
}

# --- migration-state check (library issue issue_01M1JKC0AC3ZBBA3CXC2DGSVYH):
# does the vault's alembic revision match the tree's migration head? Reads
# only — the install flow below is what decides whether to back up and
# migrate, never this check itself.

# A python3 for READ-ONLY sqlite queries only — never for building the staged
# venv (PYTHON_BIN below, found separately and held to a stricter trust rule).
# sqlite3 the CLI tool cannot be used here instead: reproduced directly, the
# `sqlite3` shell fails to open a WAL-mode database `mode=ro` once its -wal/
# -shm files are absent ("unable to open database file"), while Python's own
# `sqlite3` module with the same `mode=ro` URI reads correctly in every WAL
# state (confirmed directly; store.py's `connect()` docstring records the
# same finding for the real vault). Fixed absolute paths first, `command -v`
# only as a fallback — same shape as HASH_TOOL above, though a stale PATH
# finding the wrong python3 here only affects a diagnostic read, never a
# build, so this is not held to that search's full paranoia.
DB_READ_PYTHON=""
for cand in \
    /usr/bin/python3 \
    /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 \
    /usr/local/bin/python3
do
  if [ -x "$cand" ]; then DB_READ_PYTHON="$cand"; break; fi
done
if [ -z "$DB_READ_PYTHON" ]; then
  DB_READ_PYTHON="$(command -v python3 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi

# The sqlite3 CLI, needed only for the auto-migrate path below: `.backup`
# (which opens the live vault for real, WAL and all — unlike DB_READ_PYTHON's
# read-only URI above) and the backup's own `pragma quick_check` afterward.
# Fixed absolute paths first, `command -v` only as a fallback — same shape
# as HASH_TOOL/DB_READ_PYTHON above. Checked for existence only where it's
# actually used (an install that never reaches the migrate step needs no
# sqlite3 at all), not here.
SQLITE_BIN=""
for cand in \
    /usr/bin/sqlite3 \
    /opt/homebrew/bin/sqlite3 \
    /usr/local/bin/sqlite3
do
  if [ -x "$cand" ]; then SQLITE_BIN="$cand"; break; fi
done
if [ -z "$SQLITE_BIN" ]; then
  SQLITE_BIN="$(command -v sqlite3 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi

# Prints $1's alembic_version.version_num, read via a `mode=ro&immutable=1`
# URI, always — a write attempt on this connection raises rather than
# silently succeeding, so this can never be what touches the vault. Prints
# one of three sentinels instead of a revision when it can't:
# __DB_MISSING__ (no file at $1), __NO_VERSION_TABLE__ (opened, but alembic
# has never stamped it), or __UNREADABLE__ (any other failure — not a
# database, locked, more than one row, no python3 found). A caller must
# never treat __UNREADABLE__ or a missing python3 as "nothing pending"
# (library issue issue_01M1JKC0AC3ZBBA3CXC2DGSVYH: "a naive implementation
# will print an error that reads like 'no pending migrations'").
#
# Round 2 made this URI's `immutable=1` conditional on whether $1-wal
# existed, reading plain `mode=ro` instead when it did, on the premise that
# a live writer holding the database open was a rare, shutdown-adjacent
# event. Round 3's reviewer named the actual code path: the live service's
# own `store.connect_rw()` and every write route in app.py open a
# connection, write, commit and close it in a `finally` block PER REQUEST —
# not a connection held for the service's lifetime — so SQLite's own
# non-atomic deletion of -wal and -shm on that last-connection close happens
# on every write request, not just at shutdown. Confirmed directly: the live
# vault normally has no -wal at all, and polling a disposable database
# across repeated open-write-commit-close cycles caught `-wal` present with
# `-shm` absent in roughly a quarter of samples — a real, repeatable
# transient, not a constructed one. Round 2's plain-`mode=ro` branch, fed
# that exact state by a read landing in the gap between this script's own
# `[ -f "$db-wal" ]` check and the Python process it feeds (a process spawn,
# not the few hundred microseconds the SQLite-internal deletion spans), can
# itself CREATE a new -wal/-shm pair under the vault's own directory on a
# build where a no-wal plain `mode=ro` open succeeds rather than raising
# (Homebrew's python3, confirmed directly — the exact Linux-style build
# platform/decisions/0010 requires this script to run on) — the write this
# script's own invariant and the platform's "an install never writes state"
# rule both forbid. `immutable=1` has nothing to open that way: it never
# consults -wal and never performs the locking/journal probe a plain
# `mode=ro` open does, so it creates no file in any state — confirmed
# directly under both /usr/bin/python3 and Homebrew's python3, against a
# database with no sidecars, with -wal and -shm both present, and with -wal
# present and -shm absent.
#
# The cost of always reading `immutable=1`: it never consults -wal, so a
# migration the live service has already committed but not yet checkpointed
# into the main file reads as the OLD revision until the next checkpoint.
# Since that checkpoint runs on every write request's connection close, not
# just at shutdown, it lands within one request's lifetime of the migration
# being applied — the caller (_compute_migration_state, below) retries a
# "behind" read across that bound when -wal exists, rather than this
# function ever branching on it itself.
#
# Still raises `no such table` exactly as before — confirmed directly — so
# this changes nothing about telling that state apart from the others.
_DB_READ_PY='
import sqlite3
import sys
from urllib.parse import quote

# A raw path interpolated into a sqlite URI defeats mode=ro/immutable: the
# URI parser splits on the FIRST "?" in the whole string, so a path
# containing "?" or "#" has everything after it, directory components and
# all, swallowed as query or dropped as fragment (security-reviewer,
# convivy/library#22 — store.py.db_uri fixed the identical bug; duplicated
# rather than imported for the same reason that module gives for not
# importing it from migrate.py: this has to run under whatever interpreter
# PATH hands back, not the staged venv).
db = sys.argv[1]
encoded = quote(db, safe="/")
uri = "file:%s?mode=ro&immutable=1" % encoded
conn = None
try:
    conn = sqlite3.connect(uri, uri=True)
    rows = conn.execute("SELECT version_num FROM alembic_version").fetchall()
except sqlite3.OperationalError as exc:
    print("__NO_VERSION_TABLE__" if "no such table" in str(exc) else "__UNREADABLE__")
    sys.exit(0)
except Exception:
    print("__UNREADABLE__")
    sys.exit(0)
finally:
    if conn is not None:
        conn.close()
if len(rows) == 1:
    print(rows[0][0])
else:
    print("__UNREADABLE__")
'

_db_alembic_revision() {
  db="$1"
  [ -f "$db" ] || { echo "__DB_MISSING__"; return 0; }
  [ -n "$DB_READ_PYTHON" ] || { echo "__UNREADABLE__"; return 0; }
  "$DB_READ_PYTHON" -c "$_DB_READ_PY" "$db"
}

# The same read as _DB_READ_PY, minus the mode=ro&immutable=1 URI: a plain
# `sqlite3.connect(db)`, exactly how store.py's own connect_rw() and the
# `alembic upgrade head` call right before this one's every caller both open
# the vault, so it sees a revision alembic just committed to the WAL even
# when that commit's own checkpoint hasn't landed in the main file yet
# (alembic's own connection does the write; this one only has to see it, the
# same way any other ordinary reader of a WAL-mode database already would).
# Never used for a read that has to create nothing — the --status path and
# the pre-migration check both still read via _db_alembic_revision above —
# only for the one re-read right after this script's own `alembic upgrade
# head` has already written to the vault, where a -wal/-shm side effect is
# nothing new.
_DB_READ_LIVE_PY='
import sqlite3
import sys

db = sys.argv[1]
conn = None
try:
    conn = sqlite3.connect(db)
    rows = conn.execute("SELECT version_num FROM alembic_version").fetchall()
except sqlite3.OperationalError as exc:
    print("__NO_VERSION_TABLE__" if "no such table" in str(exc) else "__UNREADABLE__")
    sys.exit(0)
except Exception:
    print("__UNREADABLE__")
    sys.exit(0)
finally:
    if conn is not None:
        conn.close()
if len(rows) == 1:
    print(rows[0][0])
else:
    print("__UNREADABLE__")
'

_db_alembic_revision_live() {
  db="$1"
  [ -f "$db" ] || { echo "__DB_MISSING__"; return 0; }
  [ -n "$DB_READ_PYTHON" ] || { echo "__UNREADABLE__"; return 0; }
  "$DB_READ_PYTHON" -c "$_DB_READ_LIVE_PY" "$db"
}

# Finds the single alembic head among the version scripts at $1/alembic/
# versions (a directory already materialized on disk — the deployed tree for
# --status, or a throwaway `git archive` extraction of the commit being
# installed, never the checkout's own working tree). Every script's own
# `revision =` is a candidate; every script's `down_revision =` (handling a
# merge's tuple form too) removes its target from the candidate set — what's
# left is the head. Sets ALEMBIC_HEAD (the single revision) and ALEMBIC_REVS
# (every revision found, newline-separated, for the caller's own membership
# check) on success; on failure, ALEMBIC_HEAD is empty and
# ALEMBIC_HEAD_REASON says why (no directory, no scripts, or an ambiguous —
# zero or multiple — candidate set).
_alembic_head_in_dir() {
  versions_dir="$1/alembic/versions"
  ALEMBIC_HEAD=""
  ALEMBIC_REVS=""
  ALEMBIC_HEAD_REASON=""
  if [ ! -d "$versions_dir" ]; then
    ALEMBIC_HEAD_REASON="no alembic/versions directory found"
    return 1
  fi
  revs_file="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/install-library-revs.$$"
  downs_file="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/install-library-downs.$$"
  : > "$revs_file"
  : > "$downs_file"
  found_any=0
  for f in "$versions_dir"/*.py; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    found_any=1
    awk -F"['\"]" '/^revision[ \t]*=/{for (i=2;i<=NF;i+=2) print $i; exit}' "$f" >> "$revs_file"
    awk -F"['\"]" '/^down_revision[ \t]*=/{for (i=2;i<=NF;i+=2) print $i; exit}' "$f" >> "$downs_file"
  done
  if [ "$found_any" -eq 0 ]; then
    rm -f "$revs_file" "$downs_file"
    ALEMBIC_HEAD_REASON="no migration scripts found in $versions_dir"
    return 1
  fi
  ALEMBIC_REVS="$(cat "$revs_file")"
  candidates="$(grep -vFxf "$downs_file" "$revs_file" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  rm -f "$revs_file" "$downs_file"
  count="$(printf '%s\n' "$candidates" | awk 'NF{c++} END{print c+0}')"
  if [ "$count" -eq 1 ]; then
    ALEMBIC_HEAD="$candidates"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
    ALEMBIC_HEAD_REASON="no revision in $versions_dir is unreferenced as another's down_revision"
  else
    ALEMBIC_HEAD_REASON="$count candidate heads in $versions_dir (ambiguous)"
  fi
  return 1
}

# Classifies a raw _db_alembic_revision*/MIG_DB_REV reading against the
# already-computed $ALEMBIC_HEAD/$ALEMBIC_REVS, filling MIG_STATE/MIG_DB_REV.
# MIG_STATE is one of: db_missing | no_version_table | unreadable |
# revision_unknown | behind | matching. A revision present in $ALEMBIC_REVS
# but not equal to $ALEMBIC_HEAD is "behind" rather than walked through the
# down_revision chain explicitly: alembic requires a single connected chain
# to one head, and $ALEMBIC_HEAD is already confirmed unique, so any other
# known revision in the set is necessarily its ancestor. Split out so both
# _read_migration_state (below) and _read_migration_state_live (below) share
# one classification, reading $1 through whichever connection their own
# caller needs.
_classify_db_revision() {
  case "$1" in
    __DB_MISSING__) MIG_STATE="db_missing"; MIG_DB_REV=""; return 0 ;;
    __NO_VERSION_TABLE__) MIG_STATE="no_version_table"; MIG_DB_REV=""; return 0 ;;
    __UNREADABLE__) MIG_STATE="unreadable"; MIG_DB_REV=""; return 0 ;;
  esac
  MIG_DB_REV="$1"
  if [ "$MIG_DB_REV" = "$ALEMBIC_HEAD" ]; then
    MIG_STATE="matching"
  elif printf '%s\n' "$ALEMBIC_REVS" | grep -qxF "$MIG_DB_REV"; then
    MIG_STATE="behind"
  else
    MIG_STATE="revision_unknown"
  fi
}

# Reads $1 once via _db_alembic_revision (mode=ro&immutable=1 — never
# consults -wal, never creates a file) and classifies it. Used by the
# --status read and the pre-migration check, where a read must never have a
# side effect of its own. Split out of _compute_migration_state below so
# that function can call it again across the retry bound without
# duplicating this logic.
_read_migration_state() {
  _classify_db_revision "$(_db_alembic_revision "$1")"
}

# Reads $1 once via _db_alembic_revision_live (a plain connection, which
# sees the WAL) and classifies it the same way. Used ONLY right after this
# script's own `alembic upgrade head` has already written to $1: that write
# may still be sitting in the WAL, uncheckpointed, and the immutable read
# _read_migration_state uses would then report the vault as still "behind"
# on a migration that in fact succeeded (round 4 review).
_read_migration_state_live() {
  _classify_db_revision "$(_db_alembic_revision_live "$1")"
}

# Fills MIG_STATE, MIG_DB_PATH, MIG_DB_REV and MIG_WAL_RETRIED from the live
# plist's own LIBRARY_DB — read the same way this script already reads any
# plist's EnvironmentVariables, via _plist_env_pairs.
#
# A first "behind" read is retried in place, across
# INSTALL_LIBRARY_SCHEMA_RETRY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (default 2s, polled every
# INSTALL_LIBRARY_SCHEMA_RETRY_POLL_SECONDS, default 0.2s), when
# $MIG_DB_PATH-wal exists at that first read: _db_alembic_revision's
# `immutable=1` read never consults -wal, so a migration the live service
# has already committed but not yet checkpointed into the main file reads
# as the OLD revision until the next checkpoint, which the live service
# runs on every write request's connection close — within this bound, not
# at shutdown (see the comment on $_DB_READ_PY above). A -wal file that was
# never there to begin with has nothing pending to checkpoint, so that case
# is left to report "behind" at once. MIG_WAL_RETRIED records whether this
# retry ran at all, so a caller refusing on a "behind" that didn't clear can
# tell an operator the check raced a real write rather than found a genuinely
# stale vault.
_compute_migration_state() {
  MIG_DB_PATH=""
  MIG_DB_REV=""
  MIG_STATE=""
  MIG_WAL_RETRIED=0
  if [ -f "$PLIST_LIVE" ]; then
    MIG_DB_PATH="$(_plist_env_pairs "$PLIST_LIVE" | awk -F= '$1=="LIBRARY_DB"{sub(/^[^=]*=/,""); print; exit}')"
  fi
  if [ -z "$MIG_DB_PATH" ]; then
    MIG_STATE="no_library_db"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ -z "$ALEMBIC_HEAD" ]; then
    MIG_STATE="unreadable"
    return 0
  fi
  _read_migration_state "$MIG_DB_PATH"
  if [ "$MIG_STATE" = "behind" ] && [ -f "$MIG_DB_PATH-wal" ]; then
    MIG_WAL_RETRIED=1
    retry_timeout="${INSTALL_LIBRARY_SCHEMA_RETRY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-2}"
    retry_poll="${INSTALL_LIBRARY_SCHEMA_RETRY_POLL_SECONDS:-0.2}"
    retry_deadline=$(($(date +%s) + retry_timeout))
    while [ "$MIG_STATE" = "behind" ] && [ "$(date +%s)" -lt "$retry_deadline" ]; do
      sleep "$retry_poll"
      _read_migration_state "$MIG_DB_PATH"
    done
  fi
}

# Walks down_revision links backward from $ALEMBIC_HEAD (set by a prior
# _alembic_head_in_dir call) to (not including) $1 — a vault revision
# _compute_migration_state has already classified as "behind" — reading
# scripts from $2, a versions directory already materialized on disk. Sets
# PENDING_REVS and PENDING_FILES, each newline-separated and newest-pending-
# first, matched pair by line position. Returns nonzero, both left empty,
# if the walk runs out of chain (current revision empty) before reaching
# $1 — alembic's single chain must terminate there once $1 is a confirmed
# member of $ALEMBIC_REVS and $ALEMBIC_HEAD is its confirmed unique head,
# so hitting this means $2 changed underfoot since that classification, not
# a normal outcome.
_pending_migrations() {
  target="$1"
  versions_dir="$2"
  PENDING_REVS=""
  PENDING_FILES=""
  current="$ALEMBIC_HEAD"
  while [ -n "$current" ] && [ "$current" != "$target" ]; do
    found=""
    for f in "$versions_dir"/*.py; do
      [ -f "$f" ] || continue
      rv="$(awk -F"['\"]" '/^revision[ \t]*=/{for (i=2;i<=NF;i+=2) {print $i; exit}}' "$f")"
      if [ "$rv" = "$current" ]; then found="$f"; break; fi
    done
    [ -n "$found" ] || break
    if [ -z "$PENDING_REVS" ]; then PENDING_REVS="$current"; else PENDING_REVS="$PENDING_REVS
$current"; fi
    if [ -z "$PENDING_FILES" ]; then PENDING_FILES="$found"; else PENDING_FILES="$PENDING_FILES
$found"; fi
    current="$(awk -F"['\"]" '/^down_revision[ \t]*=/{for (i=2;i<=NF;i+=2) {print $i; exit}}' "$found")"
  done
  if [ "$current" != "$target" ]; then
    PENDING_REVS=""
    PENDING_FILES=""
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

# Says whether $1, a pending revision file, must block the auto-migrate
# below. Fails closed on the identifier LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY: ANY occurrence
# of it anywhere in $1 blocks UNLESS every occurrence is a plain
# module-level assignment of True (`LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY = True`, optionally
# typed `LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY: bool = True`). A one-regex match on the exact
# `= False` spelling (what this used to be) let a type-annotated False, an
# indented assignment, or a computed value through unmatched — silently
# auto-applying the one migration (the Phase 3 drop-plus-VACUUM stage) this
# flag exists to stop from ever running on an install's own timing
# (security review, PR #231 round 1). Counting occurrences rather than
# testing the first match means a file with two mentions, one safe and one
# not, still blocks.
_auto_apply_blocks_migration() {
  file="$1"
  total="$(grep -Ewc 'LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY' "$file" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [ "${total:-0}" -gt 0 ] || return 1
  safe="$(grep -Ec '^LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY([[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*bool)?[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*True[[:space:]]*$' "$file" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [ "$total" = "${safe:-0}" ] && return 1
  return 0
}

# Says whether $1 (local) is behind, ahead of, or diverged from $2 (origin's
# sha) against $REPO — used both by --status and by the branch-ref install
# path below. The comparison this replaces (a plain `[ "$SHA" != "$ORIGIN_SHA" ]`)
# already refused all three directions equally; this only says which one, so
# the operator isn't told `pull --ff-only` for an unpushed local commit a
# pull can't fix.
_describe_divergence() {
  if git -C "$REPO" merge-base --is-ancestor "$1" "$2" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "behind"
  elif git -C "$REPO" merge-base --is-ancestor "$2" "$1" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "ahead of"
  else
    echo "diverged from"
  fi
}

# Refuses — before anything is fetched or changed — unless $REPO is safe to
# fast-forward branch $1 onto origin: $1 must be the branch actually checked
# out (a merge acts on HEAD, not an arbitrary local ref — fast-forwarding a
# branch that ISN'T checked out would merge its content into whatever IS),
# no merge or rebase may already be in progress, and the working tree must
# be clean (`git status --porcelain` prints nothing). Never stashes, resets,
# checks out, or cleans anything itself — every refusal below names what's
# wrong and leaves it for the operator. Exits directly rather than
# returning, since every caller's own next step on a failure is the same:
# stop before changing anything.
_assess_checkout() {
  branch="$1"
  current_branch="$(git -C "$REPO" symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD 2>/dev/null)" || current_branch=""
  if [ "$current_branch" != "$branch" ]; then
    echo "install-library: REFUSING to pull — $REPO has '${current_branch:-a detached HEAD}' checked out, not '$branch'." >&2
    echo "  check out '$branch' yourself first, or pass --no-pull to skip this." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  # --absolute-git-dir, never --git-dir: the latter returns a path relative
  # to $REPO (because of -C), which a bare `[ -f "$git_dir/..." ]` below
  # would then resolve against THIS SCRIPT's own cwd instead.
  git_dir="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)"
  if [ -f "$git_dir/MERGE_HEAD" ]; then
    echo "install-library: REFUSING to pull — $REPO has a merge in progress (MERGE_HEAD exists)." >&2
    echo "  resolve or abort it yourself first: git -C $REPO merge --abort" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if [ -d "$git_dir/rebase-merge" ] || [ -d "$git_dir/rebase-apply" ]; then
    echo "install-library: REFUSING to pull — $REPO has a rebase in progress." >&2
    echo "  resolve or abort it yourself first: git -C $REPO rebase --abort" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  dirty="$(git -C "$REPO" status --porcelain)"
  if [ -n "$dirty" ]; then
    echo "install-library: REFUSING to pull — $REPO's working tree is not clean:" >&2
    printf '%s\n' "$dirty" | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
    echo "  commit, stash, or clean it yourself first — this never does any of that for you." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
}

# Prints, when $REPO's fetch of origin just failed, one more line naming
# refs/stash and the command to remove it — a stash pushed in ANY linked
# worktree of this repo lands at this one shared ref (library issue: on
# 2026-10-08 a dispatched run's `git stash` in a linked worktree left
# refs/stash pointing at a commit whose objects were never completed, and
# every fetch in every worktree of that repo then failed with "fatal: bad
# object refs/stash"). Never removes it — that is the operator's call, not
# this script's.
_stash_hint_on_fetch_failure() {
  if git -C "$REPO" show-ref --verify --quiet refs/stash; then
    echo "  refs/stash exists in $REPO and may be why — it is shared by every worktree of this repo, so a stash pushed in any of them lands here. Remove it with:" >&2
    echo "    git -C $REPO update-ref -d refs/stash" >&2
  fi
}

# --- --status: answer "what commit is this service serving", per ADR-0059's
# closing requirement, and exit. No install side effects. -------------------
if [ "$STATUS_ONLY" -eq 1 ]; then
  STATUS_EXIT=0
  if [ -f "$DEPLOY_DIR/DEPLOYED_COMMIT" ]; then
    cat "$DEPLOY_DIR/DEPLOYED_COMMIT"
  else
    echo "install-library: nothing deployed yet at $DEPLOY_DIR" >&2
    STATUS_EXIT=1
  fi
  if [ -f "$PLIST_RECORD" ]; then
    RECORDED_SHA="$(cat "$PLIST_RECORD")"
    echo "plist record: $RECORDED_SHA"
    if [ -f "$PLIST_LIVE" ] && [ "$(_sha256 "$PLIST_LIVE")" = "$RECORDED_SHA" ]; then
      echo "plist record matches the live plist"
    else
      echo "plist record does NOT match the live plist"
    fi
  else
    echo "plist record: none (no install has recorded one yet)"
  fi
  _alembic_head_in_dir "$DEPLOY_DIR" || true
  _compute_migration_state
  case "$MIG_STATE" in
    no_library_db)    echo "alembic db revision: unknown (LIBRARY_DB is not set in $PLIST_LIVE)" ;;
    db_missing)       echo "alembic db revision: unknown (no database file at $MIG_DB_PATH)" ;;
    no_version_table) echo "alembic db revision: unknown ($MIG_DB_PATH has no alembic_version table)" ;;
    unreadable)       echo "alembic db revision: could not be read" ;;
    *)                echo "alembic db revision: $MIG_DB_REV" ;;
  esac
  if [ -n "$ALEMBIC_HEAD" ]; then
    echo "alembic tree head: $ALEMBIC_HEAD"
  else
    echo "alembic tree head: could not be determined (${ALEMBIC_HEAD_REASON:-unknown reason})"
  fi
  case "$MIG_STATE" in
    matching)         echo "alembic: matching — the database is at the tree's head revision" ;;
    behind)           echo "alembic: BEHIND — the database is at $MIG_DB_REV, the tree's head is $ALEMBIC_HEAD" ;;
    revision_unknown) echo "alembic: the database's revision ($MIG_DB_REV) is unknown to this tree" ;;
    *)                echo "alembic: could not be determined" ;;
  esac
  if [ "$MIG_STATE" = "behind" ] && _pending_migrations "$MIG_DB_REV" "$DEPLOY_DIR/alembic/versions"; then
    echo "pending migrations (newest first):"
    printf '%s\n' "$PENDING_REVS" | sed 's/^/    /'
  fi

  if git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    if git -C "$REPO" show-ref --verify --quiet refs/heads/main; then
      LOCAL_MAIN="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse refs/heads/main)"
      if git -C "$REPO" remote get-url origin >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        if git -C "$REPO" fetch --quiet origin "+refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main" 2>/dev/null; then
          ORIGIN_MAIN="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse refs/remotes/origin/main 2>/dev/null || true)"
          if [ -n "$ORIGIN_MAIN" ]; then
            if [ "$LOCAL_MAIN" = "$ORIGIN_MAIN" ]; then
              echo "main: up to date with origin/main ($LOCAL_MAIN)"
            else
              echo "main: $(_describe_divergence "$LOCAL_MAIN" "$ORIGIN_MAIN") origin/main (local $LOCAL_MAIN, origin $ORIGIN_MAIN)"
            fi
          else
            echo "main: origin has no 'main' branch to compare against"
          fi
        else
          echo "main: could not fetch origin to compare"
        fi
      else
        echo "main: no 'origin' remote configured in $REPO"
      fi
    else
      echo "main: no local 'main' branch in $REPO"
    fi
  else
    echo "main: $REPO is not a git checkout"
  fi
  exit "$STATUS_EXIT"
fi

git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  echo "install-library: $REPO is not a git checkout" >&2
  exit 1
}

# --- refuse an ambiguous --ref outright, before any resolution runs ---------
# (security review, PR #202 round 2; round 4 adds the sixth candidate below).
# gitrevisions(7) documents six forms a bare short name falls back through,
# in order: (1) $GIT_DIR/<name> (a pseudo-ref: HEAD, FETCH_HEAD, ORIG_HEAD,
# MERGE_HEAD), (2) refs/<name>, (3) refs/tags/<name>, (4) refs/heads/<name>,
# (5) refs/remotes/<name>, (6) refs/remotes/<name>/HEAD. This loop checks
# (2) through (6) and deliberately leaves (1) uncovered: `--symbolic-full-name`
# on a pseudo-ref returns the literal pseudo-ref name itself (e.g. the string
# "FETCH_HEAD"), never a `refs/...` form and never empty, so it can land in
# neither this loop's own ambiguity check nor the `''|HEAD` arm below — it
# falls straight into the final `*)` catch-all, which refuses unconditionally
# regardless of any same-named collision (confirmed directly, round 3
# security review). A bare short name matching more than one of the five
# forms this loop DOES check is ambiguous: plain `git rev-parse` silently
# picks the first match (a tag beats a branch of the same name) and only
# warns on stderr, which this file's own `2>/dev/null` calls below would
# otherwise discard. Reproduced directly: a tag named `main` alongside
# branch `main` makes `git rev-parse main` return the TAG's sha while
# `git rev-parse --symbolic-full-name main` returns nothing — silently
# landing in the `''|HEAD` arm below with no diagnostic either way, the exact
# gap this check exists to close. Form (6) alone catches a bare name that
# collides with a configured remote's own name: `git clone` auto-creates
# refs/remotes/<remote>/HEAD, but never a bare refs/remotes/<remote> ref, so
# a branch named the same as the remote (reproduced directly: a branch named
# "origin" alongside the "origin" remote) is ambiguous by form (6) only —
# forms (2) through (5) all miss it. A fully-qualified spelling
# (refs/heads/main, refs/remotes/origin/main, refs/tags/v1) or the literal
# token HEAD already names exactly one ref by construction and needs no
# check here — HEAD in particular can never collide with a same-named branch
# or tag, since git always resolves it against $GIT_DIR/HEAD first, ahead of
# every form this loop checks.
case "$REF" in
  refs/*|HEAD) ;;
  *)
    AMBIGUOUS_REFS=""
    AMBIGUOUS_COUNT=0
    for candidate in "refs/$REF" "refs/tags/$REF" "refs/heads/$REF" "refs/remotes/$REF" "refs/remotes/$REF/HEAD"; do
      if git -C "$REPO" show-ref --verify --quiet "$candidate"; then
        AMBIGUOUS_REFS="$AMBIGUOUS_REFS
$candidate"
        AMBIGUOUS_COUNT=$((AMBIGUOUS_COUNT + 1))
      fi
    done
    # Counted directly in the loop above, never via `... | grep -c .` on the
    # accumulated string: `grep -c` exits 1 when it finds zero matching
    # lines (even though it still prints "0"), and under this script's
    # `set -eu`, a bare `VAR="$(cmd)"` assignment whose command substitution
    # itself exits nonzero aborts the whole script right there — silently,
    # with no message, since nothing had been echoed yet. Reproduced
    # directly: any --ref with zero candidates (an explicit sha, or a name
    # that resolves to nothing) hit exactly this and exited 1 with empty
    # stdout and stderr, before ever reaching this check's own refusal
    # message or the classification below it.
    if [ "$AMBIGUOUS_COUNT" -gt 1 ]; then
      echo "install-library: '$REF' is ambiguous — it could mean any of:" >&2
      printf '%s\n' "$AMBIGUOUS_REFS" | sed -n 's/^./    &/p' >&2
      echo "  pass a full ref name (one of the above) or a commit sha instead." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    ;;
esac

# --- classify what $REF RESOLVES TO, never how it's spelled -----------------
# (library issue issue_01M1VW2D48D8F4B6BM6AK28D7F). A bare branch name like
# the default "main" used to resolve against whatever the local checkout
# last pointed at, with no comparison to the remote — on 2026-09 (PR #95) and
# again on 2026-10-01 that deployed an old commit as a clean install of
# "main", because the checkout had been fetched but never fast-forwarded.
# Round 1 of this fix recognized "is $REF a branch" by matching the --ref
# STRING against refs/heads/$REF, so --ref=refs/heads/main, --ref=HEAD, and
# --ref=origin/<branch> each reached that identical gap by a different
# spelling of the same ref (round 2 review). `git rev-parse
# --symbolic-full-name` maps every spelling of the same ref to one answer:
# refs/heads/<branch> for a local branch, refs/remotes/origin/<branch> for an
# origin remote-tracking ref, refs/tags/<tag> for a tag, the literal string
# "HEAD" for a detached checkout, or an empty string for a sha (also what a
# $REF the ambiguity check above let through but that still fails to resolve
# returns — each arm's own `rev-parse` call below catches that in turn). SHA
# is (re)read from this classified full ref name inside each arm, never
# reused from a separate, earlier resolution of the bare $REF, so the commit
# verified against origin is always the exact commit that gets deployed
# (security review, PR #202 round 2).
FULL_REF="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --symbolic-full-name "$REF" 2>/dev/null)" || FULL_REF=""

HAS_ORIGIN=0
git -C "$REPO" remote get-url origin >/dev/null 2>&1 && HAS_ORIGIN=1

# True only for a full lowercase-or-uppercase hex object name (40 chars for
# sha1, 64 for sha256) — the one shape the catch-all arm below accepts as "a
# commit sha given explicitly", as opposed to an abbreviation or any other
# revision expression (main~1, a short sha) `rev-parse` would also accept but
# this installer has never documented as supported --ref input.
_looks_like_full_commit_sha() {
  case "$1" in
    *[!0-9a-fA-F]*|'') return 1 ;;
  esac
  [ "${#1}" -eq 40 ] || [ "${#1}" -eq 64 ]
}

case "$FULL_REF" in
  refs/heads/*)
    # A local branch, however $REF spelled it. With no "origin" to verify it
    # against, refuse rather than deploy it unverified (security review, PR
    # #202 round 1): a checkout missing its remote — by accident, or a bare
    # re-init — is exactly the state this guard exists to catch, not a
    # reason to skip it silently.
    BRANCH="${FULL_REF#refs/heads/}"
    if [ "$HAS_ORIGIN" -ne 1 ]; then
      echo "install-library: '$REF' names local branch '$BRANCH', which has no 'origin' remote configured to verify it against — REFUSING rather than installing it unverified." >&2
      echo "  pass a full commit sha (never a branch name) to deploy without this check." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    SHA="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse "$FULL_REF" 2>/dev/null)" || {
      echo "install-library: '$REF' (local branch '$BRANCH') does not resolve to a commit in $REPO" >&2
      exit 1
    }
    # Only the branch $REPO actually has checked out can be fast-forwarded —
    # `git merge --ff-only` acts on HEAD, so fast-forwarding a DIFFERENT
    # local branch would merge ITS content onto whatever branch is checked
    # out, not onto itself. A branch named here but not checked out (the
    # "verify a branch before merge" use case --allow-non-main's own usage
    # text names) keeps today's behaviour exactly: fetch, then refuse on any
    # mismatch in either direction, pull or no pull.
    CURRENT_BRANCH="$(git -C "$REPO" symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD 2>/dev/null)" || CURRENT_BRANCH=""
    PULLING=0
    [ "$NO_PULL" -ne 1 ] && [ "$BRANCH" = "$CURRENT_BRANCH" ] && PULLING=1
    if [ "$PULLING" -eq 1 ]; then
      _assess_checkout "$BRANCH"
    fi
    git -C "$REPO" fetch --quiet origin "+refs/heads/$BRANCH:refs/remotes/origin/$BRANCH" || {
      echo "install-library: could not fetch origin to verify local branch '$BRANCH' is current" >&2
      _stash_hint_on_fetch_failure
      exit 1
    }
    ORIGIN_SHA="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse "refs/remotes/origin/$BRANCH" 2>/dev/null)" || {
      echo "install-library: origin has no '$BRANCH' to compare against" >&2
      exit 1
    }
    if [ "$SHA" != "$ORIGIN_SHA" ]; then
      if [ "$PULLING" -eq 1 ] && git -C "$REPO" merge-base --is-ancestor "$SHA" "$ORIGIN_SHA" 2>/dev/null; then
        say "'$BRANCH' is behind origin/$BRANCH — fast-forwarding from $SHA to $ORIGIN_SHA"
        git -C "$REPO" merge --ff-only "refs/remotes/origin/$BRANCH" || {
          echo "install-library: git merge --ff-only refs/remotes/origin/$BRANCH failed in $REPO" >&2
          exit 1
        }
        SHA="$ORIGIN_SHA"
      else
        DIRECTION="$(_describe_divergence "$SHA" "$ORIGIN_SHA")"
        echo "install-library: REFUSING to install — local branch '$BRANCH' ($SHA) is $DIRECTION origin/$BRANCH ($ORIGIN_SHA)." >&2
        echo "  $REPO's local '$BRANCH' is not what origin has. Fix with:" >&2
        echo "    git -C $REPO pull --ff-only" >&2
        exit 1
      fi
    fi
    ;;
  refs/remotes/origin/*)
    # An origin remote-tracking ref, however $REF spelled it (origin/<branch>
    # or the full refs/remotes/origin/<branch> form) — fetched fresh so what
    # deploys is CURRENT, not whatever this checkout last happened to fetch.
    BRANCH="${FULL_REF#refs/remotes/origin/}"
    if [ "$HAS_ORIGIN" -ne 1 ]; then
      echo "install-library: '$REF' names origin's tracking ref for '$BRANCH', which has no 'origin' remote configured to refresh it from — REFUSING rather than installing it unverified." >&2
      echo "  pass a full commit sha (never a branch name) to deploy without this check." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    git -C "$REPO" fetch --quiet origin "+refs/heads/$BRANCH:refs/remotes/origin/$BRANCH" || {
      echo "install-library: could not fetch origin to refresh '$REF'" >&2
      _stash_hint_on_fetch_failure
      exit 1
    }
    SHA="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse "refs/remotes/origin/$BRANCH" 2>/dev/null)" || {
      echo "install-library: origin has no '$BRANCH' to install" >&2
      exit 1
    }
    say "'$REF' refreshed from origin: installing $SHA"
    ;;
  refs/tags/*)
    # A tag. The ambiguity check above already ruled out a same-named branch
    # for a bare spelling; a fully-qualified refs/tags/... spelling is
    # unambiguous by construction. Not fetched or compared — this installer
    # cannot fetch and compare a tag against a branch, so it deploys exactly
    # as given, same as before this fix.
    SHA="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse "$FULL_REF" 2>/dev/null)" || {
      echo "install-library: '$REF' (tag '$FULL_REF') does not resolve to a commit in $REPO" >&2
      exit 1
    }
    ;;
  ''|HEAD)
    # A full commit sha, or a detached HEAD (reported as the literal string
    # "HEAD") — neither names a branch this installer can fetch and compare,
    # so each deploys exactly as given, same as before this fix. Anything
    # else landing here (an abbreviated sha, a relative expression like
    # main~1) is refused rather than silently joining this unverified-deploy
    # path (security review, PR #202 round 2): only a positively-identified
    # full sha, or the detached-HEAD case, reaches the `rev-parse` below.
    if [ "$FULL_REF" != "HEAD" ] && ! _looks_like_full_commit_sha "$REF"; then
      echo "install-library: '$REF' does not resolve to a branch, a tag, or a full commit sha this installer recognizes — refusing rather than guessing what to deploy." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    SHA="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse "$REF" 2>/dev/null)" || {
      echo "install-library: '$REF' does not resolve to a commit in $REPO" >&2
      exit 1
    }
    ;;
  *)
    # $REF resolved to something this installer does not know how to
    # classify (e.g. a ref outside refs/heads, refs/remotes/origin and
    # refs/tags) — refused rather than falling through to an unchecked
    # deploy by default.
    echo "install-library: '$REF' resolves to '$FULL_REF', which install-library does not know how to classify — refusing rather than deploying it unchecked." >&2
    exit 1
    ;;
esac

if [ "$REF" != "main" ] && [ "$ALLOW_NON_MAIN" -ne 1 ]; then
  echo "install-library: REFUSING to install ref '$REF' (resolves to $SHA)." >&2
  echo "  ADR-0059 rule 6: the deploy area installs from main. Pass --allow-non-main" >&2
  echo "  to install a different ref anyway (e.g. to verify a branch before merge)." >&2
  exit 1
fi

# --- refuse to stage a vault behind $SHA's migration head (library issue
# issue_01M1JKC0AC3ZBBA3CXC2DGSVYH): ahead of EVERYTHING else below — nothing
# has been staged, no plist has been read or compared, no launchd call has
# been made — because the hazard this guards is the running service itself
# quiescing and restarting onto a staged commit within seconds of the swap
# below, unprompted and with nobody watching (the 2026-09-04 and 2026-09-10
# incidents this issue records: on a live job, `install-library` printing
# "nothing was loaded, unloaded, kickstarted, or restarted" did not mean
# nothing was about to happen).
#
# Skipped entirely when $PLIST_LIVE does not exist at all: with no job
# loaded from a prior install, there is no running service that could
# self-restart out from under an operator, so there is nothing here for this
# gate to protect — the very first install onto a machine already requires
# the vault to exist and be stamped by hand (store.py's baseline migration,
# `alembic/versions/0b1e7c4a9d52_baseline_the_migrated_vaults.py`), outside
# this installer's own scope.
#
# The tree's migration head is read from $SHA via `git archive`, never this
# checkout's working tree (library issue issue_01M1JKC0AC3ZBBA3CXC2DGSVYH:
# "Determine the tree's head from the alembic scripts in the commit being
# installed") — the same principle as the code staged below, in a scratch
# directory removed right after this check reads it.
if [ -f "$PLIST_LIVE" ]; then
  ALEMBIC_SCRATCH="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/install-library-alembic-scratch.$$"
  rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
  mkdir -p "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
  git -C "$REPO" archive "$SHA" -- alembic/versions 2>/dev/null | tar -x -C "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH" 2>/dev/null || true
  _alembic_head_in_dir "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH" || true
  _compute_migration_state

  # --- auto-migrate: only for "behind", never for a state where the vault's
  # own revision is unknown or unreadable (an unknown revision usually means
  # the vault is AHEAD of the tree — a rollback — which migrating would make
  # worse, not better). $ALEMBIC_SCRATCH is kept alive through this whole
  # block (not removed right after _alembic_head_in_dir, as it is on a run
  # that never migrates) because _pending_migrations below reads the same
  # version scripts a second time to walk the chain and check each one for
  # LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY. -----------------------------------------------------
  if [ "$MIG_STATE" = "behind" ] && [ "$NO_MIGRATE" -ne 1 ] && [ "$ALLOW_SCHEMA_BEHIND" -ne 1 ]; then
    if ! _pending_migrations "$MIG_DB_REV" "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH/alembic/versions"; then
      echo "install-library: REFUSING to install $SHA — could not walk the migration chain from $MIG_DB_REV to ${ALEMBIC_HEAD:-unknown} to find the pending revisions." >&2
      rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
      exit 1
    fi
    BLOCKED_FILE=""
    while IFS= read -r pf; do
      [ -n "$pf" ] || continue
      if _auto_apply_blocks_migration "$pf"; then
        BLOCKED_FILE="$pf"
        break
      fi
    done <<PENDING_FILES_EOF
$PENDING_FILES
PENDING_FILES_EOF

    if [ -n "$BLOCKED_FILE" ]; then
      echo "install-library: REFUSING to migrate — $(basename "$BLOCKED_FILE") mentions LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY without a plain 'LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY = True' (see alembic/README)." >&2
      echo "  run it by hand before the next install:" >&2
      echo "    LIBRARY_DB=$MIG_DB_PATH $REPO/.venv/bin/python -m alembic upgrade head" >&2
      rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
      exit 1
    fi

    [ -n "$SQLITE_BIN" ] || {
      echo "install-library: sqlite3 not found (checked /usr/bin, /opt/homebrew/bin, /usr/local/bin, and PATH) — cannot back up $MIG_DB_PATH to migrate it." >&2
      rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
      exit 1
    }
    SHORT_SHA="$(git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --short "$SHA" 2>/dev/null)" || SHORT_SHA="$SHA"
    BACKUP_DEST="$MIG_DB_PATH.pre-deploy-$SHORT_SHA-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)"
    say "The vault is behind ($MIG_DB_REV, tree head ${ALEMBIC_HEAD:-unknown}) — backing it up to $BACKUP_DEST"
    # sqlite3's own `.backup` dot-command, never `cp`: the vault runs in WAL
    # mode, and a copy of the main file alone silently loses uncheckpointed
    # writes `.backup` would not.
    if ! "$SQLITE_BIN" "$MIG_DB_PATH" ".backup '$BACKUP_DEST'"; then
      echo "install-library: REFUSING to install $SHA — could not back up $MIG_DB_PATH to $BACKUP_DEST" >&2
      rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
      exit 1
    fi
    # `sqlite3 -readonly` cannot open a WAL backup without a live writer; the
    # same `mode=ro&immutable=1` URI this script already uses to read the
    # vault's own revision opens it correctly in every WAL state.
    BACKUP_CHECK="$("$SQLITE_BIN" "file:$BACKUP_DEST?immutable=1" 'pragma quick_check' 2>&1)" || true
    if [ "$BACKUP_CHECK" != "ok" ]; then
      echo "install-library: REFUSING to install $SHA — the backup at $BACKUP_DEST failed its own integrity check: $BACKUP_CHECK" >&2
      rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
      exit 1
    fi
    say "Backup verified ok — running alembic upgrade head from $REPO"
    # From $REPO, never the deploy area: the deploy area's own alembic/
    # versions still holds the CURRENTLY deployed revisions until the
    # install below extracts the new tree, so `upgrade head` run there
    # would resolve against the old graph. python -m alembic, never the
    # `alembic` console script, so this runs under the same interpreter
    # $REPO's own venv was built with.
    if ! ( cd "$REPO" && LIBRARY_DB="$MIG_DB_PATH" "$REPO/.venv/bin/python" -m alembic upgrade head ); then
      echo "install-library: REFUSING to install $SHA — alembic upgrade head failed. The vault's pre-migration backup is kept at:" >&2
      echo "    $BACKUP_DEST" >&2
      rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
      exit 1
    fi
    _read_migration_state_live "$MIG_DB_PATH"
    if [ "$MIG_STATE" != "matching" ]; then
      echo "install-library: REFUSING to install $SHA — after alembic upgrade head, the vault reads '$MIG_STATE' ($MIG_DB_REV), not the tree's head (${ALEMBIC_HEAD:-unknown}). The vault's pre-migration backup is kept at:" >&2
      echo "    $BACKUP_DEST" >&2
      rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"
      exit 1
    fi
    say "Migrated: the vault is now at $MIG_DB_REV"
  fi
  rm -rf "$ALEMBIC_SCRATCH"

  if [ "$MIG_STATE" != "matching" ] && [ "$ALLOW_SCHEMA_BEHIND" -ne 1 ]; then
    echo "install-library: REFUSING to install $SHA — the vault's schema state is not confirmed safe." >&2
    case "$MIG_STATE" in
      no_library_db)
        echo "  LIBRARY_DB is not set in the live plist ($PLIST_LIVE) — there is no database to check." >&2 ;;
      db_missing)
        echo "  no database file at $MIG_DB_PATH." >&2 ;;
      no_version_table)
        echo "  $MIG_DB_PATH has no alembic_version table (alembic has never stamped it)." >&2 ;;
      unreadable)
        echo "  the vault's alembic revision at $MIG_DB_PATH could not be read." >&2 ;;
      revision_unknown)
        echo "  the vault is at revision $MIG_DB_REV, which $SHA's migration scripts do not recognize." >&2 ;;
      behind)
        echo "  the vault is at revision $MIG_DB_REV; $SHA's migration head is ${ALEMBIC_HEAD:-unknown}." >&2
        if [ "$MIG_WAL_RETRIED" -eq 1 ]; then
          echo "  a -wal file was present and this was re-read for about ${retry_timeout:-2} seconds without the revision changing — re-run this check, since an in-progress write can still be mid-checkpoint." >&2
        fi
        if [ "$NO_MIGRATE" -eq 1 ]; then
          echo "  --no-migrate was given, so this was never attempted automatically." >&2
        fi
        ;;
    esac
    if [ -n "$MIG_DB_PATH" ]; then
      echo "  back up the vault, then apply the pending migration(s):" >&2
      echo "    sqlite3 $MIG_DB_PATH \".backup '$MIG_DB_PATH.pre-deploy-\$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)'\"" >&2
      echo "    LIBRARY_DB=$MIG_DB_PATH $REPO/.venv/bin/python -m alembic upgrade head" >&2
    fi
    echo "  pass --allow-schema-behind to install anyway." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if [ "$MIG_STATE" != "matching" ]; then
    warn "installing with the vault's schema state as '$MIG_STATE' — --allow-schema-behind was given"
  fi
fi

# --- find a real interpreter, never one a poisoned PATH could hand back ----
# A checkout's own .venv/bin can sit ahead of the system paths on PATH (true
# of the shell that built this script), so `command -v python3.11` is tried
# LAST, after the well-known absolute locations, and only as a fallback.
#
# --python=<path> skips this search entirely and takes the given interpreter
# on faith to BUILD the venv with — it does not touch the trust guard below.
# The staged build still has to pass the deployed serve.sh's own ownership
# walk via `--check` before the swap (rule 4), same as every other install,
# so an untrusted --python cannot reach a live swap; it can only fail its own
# staged build's preflight. Every production invocation omits this and gets
# the exact same hardcoded-path search as before.
if [ -n "$PYTHON_OVERRIDE" ]; then
  [ -x "$PYTHON_OVERRIDE" ] || {
    echo "install-library: --python=$PYTHON_OVERRIDE is not an executable file" >&2
    exit 1
  }
  PYTHON_BIN="$PYTHON_OVERRIDE"
else
  PYTHON_BIN=""
  for cand in \
      /opt/homebrew/bin/python3.11 \
      /usr/local/bin/python3.11 \
      /usr/bin/python3.11
  do
    if [ -x "$cand" ]; then PYTHON_BIN="$cand"; break; fi
  done
  if [ -z "$PYTHON_BIN" ]; then
    PYTHON_BIN="$(command -v python3.11 2>/dev/null || true)"
  fi
  [ -n "$PYTHON_BIN" ] || {
    echo "install-library: python3.11 not found (checked /opt/homebrew/bin," >&2
    echo "  /usr/local/bin, /usr/bin, and PATH)" >&2
    exit 1
  }
fi

say "Installing the Library from $REF ($SHA) in $REPO, using $PYTHON_BIN"

# --- decide, BEFORE anything else, whether this install will change what's
# live — reading only `git show`, never the working tree, same as rule 1.
# This has to run before the code is staged: the plist this install would
# write is compared against the live one right here, and if it differs (and
# --force allows the write), the job comes down NOW, before a single byte of
# the new release exists on disk (security review, PR #157 round 1). Neither
# "old code, new environment" nor "new code, old environment" can happen if
# the job is never running while both are true — that's the whole reason this
# comes first rather than after the swap. -------------------------------------
mkdir -p "$LAUNCH_AGENTS"
PLIST_CANDIDATE="$LAUNCH_AGENTS/$PLIST_NAME.candidate.$$"
# The template read from git still carries $PLIST_HOME_PLACEHOLDER, never a
# literal home directory — rendered here, against this run's own $HOME,
# before the candidate is compared against anything or written anywhere.
# Drift, the sha256 record, and the live file all see only rendered content
# from this point on; none of them ever reads the raw template. Two steps
# rather than a pipe: this /bin/sh has no pipefail, so a failed `git show`
# piped into the renderer would leave an empty candidate instead of
# stopping the install under `set -e`.
git -C "$REPO" show "$SHA:launchd/$PLIST_NAME" > "$PLIST_CANDIDATE"
_render_plist "$PLIST_CANDIDATE" > "$PLIST_CANDIDATE.rendered"
mv "$PLIST_CANDIDATE.rendered" "$PLIST_CANDIDATE"

LABEL="${PLIST_NAME%.plist}"
LAUNCHD_DOMAIN="${INSTALL_LIBRARY_LAUNCHD_DOMAIN:-gui/$(id -u)}"

# Drift is decided against $PLIST_RECORD — what THIS installer itself last
# put at $PLIST_LIVE — never against the candidate template. Deciding it
# against the template refused every shipped plist change as drift, since a
# legitimate template change and an operator's hand edit look identical from
# the template's side (library issue 01M3N7NH8F73WTKPN8WQGFZ3E2). A live
# plist matching the record means the template is free to change under it,
# reload included, with no --force; a live plist that does NOT match the
# record — including when no record exists yet at all — is operator drift,
# refused exactly as before.
SAME_AS_CANDIDATE=0
if [ -f "$PLIST_LIVE" ] && diff -q "$PLIST_LIVE" "$PLIST_CANDIDATE" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  SAME_AS_CANDIDATE=1
fi

IS_DRIFT=0
if [ -f "$PLIST_LIVE" ] && [ "$SAME_AS_CANDIDATE" -ne 1 ]; then
  if [ -f "$PLIST_RECORD" ] && [ "$(_sha256 "$PLIST_LIVE")" = "$(cat "$PLIST_RECORD")" ]; then
    IS_DRIFT=0
  else
    IS_DRIFT=1
  fi
fi

PLIST_REFUSED=0
PLIST_WILL_WRITE=0
if [ "$IS_DRIFT" -eq 1 ] && [ "$PLIST_FORCE" -ne 1 ]; then
  PLIST_REFUSED=1
  if [ -f "$PLIST_RECORD" ]; then
    RECORDED_SHA_MSG="$(cat "$PLIST_RECORD" 2>/dev/null || echo '?')"
  else
    RECORDED_SHA_MSG="none (no prior install recorded one)"
  fi
  warn "live plist differs from what install-library last installed — NOT overwritten (--force to replace):"
  warn "  recorded sha256: $RECORDED_SHA_MSG"
  warn "  live sha256:     $(_sha256 "$PLIST_LIVE")"
  diff -u "$PLIST_LIVE" "$PLIST_CANDIDATE" | sed 's/^/    /' || true
  rm -f "$PLIST_CANDIDATE"
elif [ "$SAME_AS_CANDIDATE" -ne 1 ]; then
  PLIST_WILL_WRITE=1
fi

# ============================================================================
# THE RELOADING INSTALL, STEP BY STEP. Only taken when PLIST_WILL_WRITE=1 (a
# plist write that wasn't refused as drift). In order: bootout, retain $OLD,
# stage the new tree, write the new plist, bootstrap, verify, clean up $OLD
# and the backup. (The new tree is actually BUILT in a sibling directory,
# $STAGE, while the previous release is still sitting at $DEPLOY_DIR and the
# job is down — "retain $OLD" is what the swap step does with that previous
# release once the staged build has already passed its own preflight, not a
# step that runs before staging starts.)
#
# Every step's failure ends in exactly one of two outcomes:
#   (i)  RESTORED — the previous release tree and plist are bootstrapped and
#        verified as a pair, and $PLIST_LIVE is rewritten to match what's
#        actually loaded.
#   (ii) FAIL CLOSED — nothing is loaded; every file the exit message names
#        is kept on disk, never deleted by the message that names it.
#
#   1. BOOTOUT. On disk: the previous release is still at $DEPLOY_DIR, the
#      previous plist is still at $PLIST_LIVE ($PLIST_BACKUP is a copy of
#      it, taken here). Loaded: nothing — `bootout`'s own exit status is
#      ignored (`|| true`): a job that was never loaded is not an error, and
#      nothing downstream depends on this call having succeeded.
#
#   2. STAGE THE NEW TREE (git archive, venv build, serve.sh --check) into
#      $STAGE. On disk: $DEPLOY_DIR is untouched, $STAGE holds the
#      candidate. Loaded: nothing. Recovery: outcome (i) if a previous
#      plist backup exists (nothing to restore in the tree — it was never
#      touched — so only the job needs bringing back up); outcome (ii)
#      otherwise (there was nothing to restore to).
#
#   3. RETAIN $OLD, SWAP. $DEPLOY_DIR is renamed to $OLD, not deleted, then
#      $STAGE is moved into $DEPLOY_DIR. Each `mv` here is guarded
#      explicitly: if the second one fails, this tries to undo the first on
#      the spot, moving $OLD back into $DEPLOY_DIR — but that restoring `mv`
#      can itself fail (round 4 review, PR #157), and when it does, this
#      does NOT claim the previous release was restored and does NOT clear
#      $OLD: it fails closed, naming $OLD's actual path in the exit message,
#      and $DEPLOY_DIR IS left empty until that path is moved back by hand.
#      On disk after a clean swap: $DEPLOY_DIR holds the NEW release, $OLD
#      the PREVIOUS one. Loaded: still nothing. Recovery: outcome (i), the
#      pair-restore in the trap below — this is the code-swap step SWAP_DONE
#      guards, so a failure here (either `mv`) is never seen as SWAP_DONE by
#      the trap, and none of its own code-restore logic runs a second time
#      for what this step already tried and reported on directly.
#
#   4. WRITE THE NEW PLIST. $PLIST_CANDIDATE (already diffed against the
#      live one, above) is moved onto $PLIST_LIVE. On disk: $PLIST_LIVE now
#      holds the NEW plist's bytes. Loaded: still nothing. Recovery: outcome
#      (i), the pair-restore below.
#
#   5. BOOTSTRAP. `launchctl bootstrap` is handed $PLIST_LIVE. On disk:
#      unchanged. Loaded: launchd has ACCEPTED the definition — not proof
#      the process is up. A nonzero exit here means it was refused outright;
#      nothing is loaded. Recovery: outcome (i), the pair-restore below —
#      this is the LAST step where auto-recovery is attempted.
#
#   6. VERIFY. Bounded retries of `launchctl print`, confirming `state =
#      running`, every EnvironmentVariables pair the new plist sets, and that
#      the EFFECTIVE `exit timeout` it reports exceeds app.py's
#      `_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS` (issue_01M3N7NB9218HXKQDPD3NF1BJH: the
#      plist's own `ExitTimeOut` integer is not what launchd applies). On
#      disk: unchanged. Loaded: the new pair MAY already be running by now.
#      Once step 5 returned 0, a verify failure here does NOT trigger
#      auto-recovery: the new process may already be up, and bootstrapping
#      the old pair over it a second time, unasked, is not a safe default
#      (round 1 review). This is outcome (ii), but unlike every other FAIL
#      CLOSED case, "nothing is loaded" isn't certain — $PLIST_LIVE (new
#      bytes) and $OLD (kept) are both left exactly as they are, and the
#      exit message gives commands for both directions: retry forward with
#      $PLIST_LIVE, or roll back by hand to $OLD and $PLIST_BACKUP.
#
#   7. CLEAN UP. Only reached once step 6 verifies. $PLIST_BACKUP and $OLD
#      are removed — the new pair is confirmed loaded and correct.
# ============================================================================
RELOADING=0
PRESERVE_FOR_OPERATOR=0
PLIST_BACKUP=""
OLD=""
SWAP_DONE=0
if [ "$PLIST_WILL_WRITE" -eq 1 ]; then
  RELOADING=1
  if [ -f "$PLIST_LIVE" ]; then
    PLIST_BACKUP="$PLIST_LIVE.pre-reload.$$"
    cp "$PLIST_LIVE" "$PLIST_BACKUP"
  fi
  say "Plist will change — taking $LABEL down before staging the new release"
  "$LAUNCHCTL" bootout "$LAUNCHD_DOMAIN/$LABEL" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
fi

# Verifies $1 (a plist path) against `launchctl print`: the job must read
# `state = running`, every KEY=VALUE pair $1 sets under EnvironmentVariables
# must appear, unaltered, in print's own environment block (via
# _launchctl_env_actual_pairs above — never the inherited or default
# blocks), and — the EFFECTIVE ExitTimeOut check, not the plist's own number
# (issue_01M3N7NB9218HXKQDPD3NF1BJH: a plist that reads 75 was clamped by
# launchd itself to 60, so the file's own integer proves nothing about what
# launchd applies) — print's own `exit timeout` must exceed $DEPLOY_DIR/
# app.py's `_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS`, read once via _drain_deadline_seconds
# above rather than a second copy of that number in this script. FAILS
# CLOSED on either side of that comparison: an empty `DRAIN_DEADLINE` (no
# top-level `_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS` in app.py, or a value that isn't a
# plain integer once _drain_deadline_seconds strips whitespace, the type
# annotation and a trailing comment) or an empty `EXIT_TIMEOUT_ACTUAL` on a
# running job (no `exit timeout` line in `launchctl print`'s own output)
# fails verification exactly like a genuinely insufficient value would — a
# real deploy area and a real launchd always have both, so an unreadable
# side here is never a fixture exercising something else, only a broken
# read to report as one (round 1 review, PR #159: this used to skip the
# comparison silently and verify anyway). EXIT_TIMEOUT_INSUFFICIENT itself
# starts at 1 and is cleared only by a `[ ... -gt ... ]` that both runs and
# is true — a digit string too large for `[` to compare (both sides are
# checked digits-only, not bounded in size) makes `[` print its own error
# and return false, which leaves the flag at 1 exactly like a true
# insufficient value would, rather than at its old default of 0 (round 2
# review, PR #159: a comparison `[` cannot make used to leave this flag
# unset and verify anyway). The caller's own failure message tells the two
# unreadable cases apart from each other and from a value that read but was
# either insufficient or too large to compare, and names where each one
# looked.
# Retries on a bound (INSTALL_LIBRARY_RELOAD_VERIFY_{TIMEOUT,POLL}_SECONDS,
# 30s/2s in production) since launchd can take a moment after `bootstrap`
# returns before `print` reflects the new state. Sets JOB_STATE, PRINT_OK,
# MISSING, EXIT_TIMEOUT_ACTUAL, DRAIN_DEADLINE, DRAIN_DEADLINE_UNREADABLE,
# EXIT_TIMEOUT_UNREADABLE, EXIT_TIMEOUT_INSUFFICIENT and VERIFY_TIMEOUT for
# the caller's own failure message; returns 0/1 rather than printing
# anything itself. Shared between the primary reload (step 6 above) and the
# recovery trap's own pair-restore below — one definition, not two copies to
# keep in sync.
_verify_reload() {
  verify_plist="$1"
  VERIFY_TIMEOUT="${INSTALL_LIBRARY_RELOAD_VERIFY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-30}"
  VERIFY_POLL="${INSTALL_LIBRARY_RELOAD_VERIFY_POLL_SECONDS:-2}"
  env_pairs_file="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/install-library-env-pairs.$$"
  _plist_env_pairs "$verify_plist" > "$env_pairs_file"
  DRAIN_DEADLINE="$(_drain_deadline_seconds "$DEPLOY_DIR/app.py")"
  case "$DRAIN_DEADLINE" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*) DRAIN_DEADLINE_UNREADABLE=1 ;;
    *) DRAIN_DEADLINE_UNREADABLE=0 ;;
  esac

  VERIFIED=0
  deadline=$(($(date +%s) + VERIFY_TIMEOUT))
  while :; do
    JOB_STATE="$("$LAUNCHCTL" print "$LAUNCHD_DOMAIN/$LABEL" 2>&1)" && PRINT_OK=1 || PRINT_OK=0
    MISSING=""
    EXIT_TIMEOUT_ACTUAL=""
    EXIT_TIMEOUT_UNREADABLE=0
    EXIT_TIMEOUT_INSUFFICIENT=1
    if [ "$PRINT_OK" -eq 1 ]; then
      case "$JOB_STATE" in
        *"state = running"*)
          actual_env_file="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/install-library-actual-env.$$"
          printf '%s\n' "$JOB_STATE" | _launchctl_env_actual_pairs > "$actual_env_file"
          while IFS='=' read -r k v; do
            [ -n "$k" ] || continue
            if ! grep -qxF -- "$k=$v" "$actual_env_file"; then
              MISSING="$MISSING $k"
            fi
          done < "$env_pairs_file"
          rm -f "$actual_env_file"
          EXIT_TIMEOUT_ACTUAL="$(printf '%s\n' "$JOB_STATE" | \
            sed -n 's/^[ 	]*exit timeout = \([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p' | head -n 1)"
          [ -z "$EXIT_TIMEOUT_ACTUAL" ] && EXIT_TIMEOUT_UNREADABLE=1
          if [ "$EXIT_TIMEOUT_UNREADABLE" -eq 0 ] && [ "$DRAIN_DEADLINE_UNREADABLE" -eq 0 ] && \
             [ "$EXIT_TIMEOUT_ACTUAL" -gt "$DRAIN_DEADLINE" ] 2>/dev/null; then
            EXIT_TIMEOUT_INSUFFICIENT=0
          fi
          [ -z "$MISSING" ] && [ "$EXIT_TIMEOUT_UNREADABLE" -eq 0 ] && \
            [ "$DRAIN_DEADLINE_UNREADABLE" -eq 0 ] && [ "$EXIT_TIMEOUT_INSUFFICIENT" -eq 0 ] && \
            VERIFIED=1
          ;;
      esac
    fi
    [ "$VERIFIED" -eq 1 ] && break
    [ "$(date +%s)" -lt "$deadline" ] || break
    sleep "$VERIFY_POLL"
  done
  rm -f "$env_pairs_file"
  [ "$VERIFIED" -eq 1 ]
}

# --- recovery trap. Reachable on any nonzero exit while RELOADING=1.
#
# If PRESERVE_FOR_OPERATOR was set before the exit (step 6's verify-timeout,
# the one case where auto-recovery is deliberately not attempted — see the
# step table above), this does only the shared candidate cleanup plus one
# more thing: $PLIST_LIVE already holds the new plist's bytes at this point
# (step 6 is only reached after that mv succeeded), so the record is
# rewritten to match it, same as every other recovery outcome below — a
# reader comparing $PLIST_RECORD to $PLIST_LIVE right after this exit must
# see them agree, not a record still describing whatever plist was live
# before this install ran (round 2 review, PR #158). $OLD and $PLIST_BACKUP
# are left exactly as this exit path already named them.
#
# Otherwise ($LABEL's own bootstrap was never issued, or was refused
# outright), this attempts outcome (i): move $DEPLOY_DIR's failed contents
# aside, move $OLD back into place, bootstrap $PLIST_BACKUP, and verify it —
# the SAME verify step 6 uses, not a second copy. If that whole chain
# succeeds, $PLIST_LIVE is rewritten to match (rule: the file on disk must
# never disagree with what's loaded, `launchd/com.convivy.library.plist`'s
# own comment on this exact hazard) — and the plist RECORD is rewritten
# right after, whether or not that rewrite of $PLIST_LIVE itself succeeded,
# so the record can never disagree with whatever bytes are actually sitting
# at $PLIST_LIVE (round 1 review, PR #158: a failed rewrite used to leave the
# record at its pre-reload value, stale against the file it's compared to).
# If any part of it fails, this ends in
# outcome (ii): the job is bootout'd (in case a partial bootstrap took),
# $PLIST_LIVE is removed (it no longer describes anything loaded — leaving
# it would fool a LATER install's own drift-diff into thinking nothing
# changed, round 3 review), and the message names $OLD and/or $PLIST_BACKUP
# with the exact commands to load them by hand. Neither is ever deleted in
# this branch.
#
# Every filesystem operation below is checked explicitly, on its own exit
# status, never on `set -e` doing it implicitly — `set +e` is this
# function's own first executed statement (after `st=$?`, which has to come
# first or the capture itself is what `set +e`'s own exit status clobbers).
# Round 3 review already found `set -e` an unreliable way to stop this
# function early on this /bin/sh, bash 3.2.57; round 4 (PR #157) found the
# fix incomplete — four `rm` calls below were still bare, each one able to
# abort the trap mid-message on a real failure, taking the RECOVERY FAILED
# text and the correct exit status with it. `set +e` closes that off
# structurally rather than leaving it to how reliably this bash honors an
# `if`/`||`'s protected-context exemption while already inside a running
# trap. The previous release is always moved aside, never deleted, until its
# replacement is confirmed in place.
_recover_failed_reload() {
  st=$?
  set +e
  rm -f "$PLIST_CANDIDATE" 2>/dev/null || true

  if [ "$PRESERVE_FOR_OPERATOR" -eq 1 ]; then
    _write_plist_record "$PLIST_LIVE"
    exit "$st"
  fi
  if [ "$st" -eq 0 ] || [ "$RELOADING" -ne 1 ]; then
    rm -f "$PLIST_BACKUP" 2>/dev/null || true
    exit "$st"
  fi

  warn "install failed after taking $LABEL down for reload"

  if [ -z "$PLIST_BACKUP" ] || [ ! -f "$PLIST_BACKUP" ]; then
    warn "$LABEL was not previously loaded — nothing to restore, it stays down"
    if [ "$SWAP_DONE" -eq 1 ] && [ -f "$PLIST_LIVE" ]; then
      if [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_TRAP_RM:-0}" = "1" ] || ! rm -f "$PLIST_LIVE"; then
        warn "could not remove stale $PLIST_LIVE — remove it by hand before the next install"
      fi
    fi
    exit "$st"
  fi

  code_restored=1
  if [ "$SWAP_DONE" -eq 1 ]; then
    if [ -n "$OLD" ] && [ -d "$OLD" ]; then
      BROKEN="$DEPLOY_DIR.broken.$$"
      if ! mv "$DEPLOY_DIR" "$BROKEN"; then
        code_restored=0
      # INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_RECOVERY_SWAP is a test-only seam
      # (tests/test_install_library.py) that forces this specific `mv` to
      # fail without needing a real filesystem-level failure — everything
      # after the `||` is the real guarded recovery path either way. The
      # same seam also forces the primary swap's own restoring `mv` further
      # up the script (identical command, `mv "$OLD" "$DEPLOY_DIR"`), so a
      # test that sets it exercises whichever of the two is actually reached.
      elif [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_RECOVERY_SWAP:-0}" = "1" ] || ! mv "$OLD" "$DEPLOY_DIR"; then
        mv "$BROKEN" "$DEPLOY_DIR" 2>/dev/null || true
        code_restored=0
      else
        if [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_TRAP_RM:-0}" = "1" ] || ! rm -rf "$BROKEN"; then
          warn "could not remove leftover $BROKEN — the restored release at $DEPLOY_DIR is unaffected; remove it by hand"
        fi
        OLD=""
      fi
    else
      code_restored=0
    fi
  fi

  if [ "$code_restored" -eq 1 ] && \
     "$LAUNCHCTL" bootstrap "$LAUNCHD_DOMAIN" "$PLIST_BACKUP" >/dev/null 2>&1 && \
     _verify_reload "$PLIST_BACKUP"; then
    # INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_RECOVERY_CP forces this `cp` to fail without
    # needing a real filesystem-level failure — see the record-write below,
    # which runs either way so the record can never go stale relative to
    # whatever is actually at $PLIST_LIVE afterward (round 1 review, PR #158).
    if [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_RECOVERY_CP:-0}" != "1" ] && cp "$PLIST_BACKUP" "$PLIST_LIVE" 2>/dev/null; then
      warn "restored: $LABEL bootstrapped from the previous release and plist"
      if [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_TRAP_RM:-0}" = "1" ] || ! rm -f "$PLIST_BACKUP"; then
        warn "restored and $PLIST_LIVE matches, but stale backup $PLIST_BACKUP could not be removed — remove it by hand"
      fi
    else
      warn "restored: $LABEL bootstrapped from the previous release and plist," \
           "but $PLIST_LIVE could not be rewritten to match — it IS loaded and" \
           "correct; fix the file by hand: cp $PLIST_BACKUP $PLIST_LIVE"
    fi
    # Always records what's actually at $PLIST_LIVE now, whether or not the
    # cp above landed — the record must never disagree with the file on
    # disk, even when that file (still the failed reload's own candidate)
    # disagrees with what's loaded; the warning above already tells the
    # operator that part by hand (round 1 review, PR #158: a failed cp used
    # to leave the record at its pre-reload value, silently mismatched
    # against the candidate bytes still sitting at $PLIST_LIVE).
    _write_plist_record "$PLIST_LIVE"
    exit "$st"
  fi

  "$LAUNCHCTL" bootout "$LAUNCHD_DOMAIN/$LABEL" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  if [ -f "$PLIST_LIVE" ]; then
    if [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_TRAP_RM:-0}" = "1" ] || ! rm -f "$PLIST_LIVE"; then
      warn "could not remove stale $PLIST_LIVE — remove it by hand before the next install"
    fi
  fi
  echo "install-library: RECOVERY FAILED — $LABEL is down." >&2
  if [ "$code_restored" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "    could not restore the previous release tree to $DEPLOY_DIR" >&2
    if [ -n "$OLD" ] && [ -d "$OLD" ]; then
      echo "    (the new release is still there); the previous one is intact at:" >&2
      echo "      $OLD" >&2
      echo "    move it back by hand, then retry:" >&2
      echo "      mv $OLD $DEPLOY_DIR" >&2
    else
      echo "    and no previous release was retained to restore it from" >&2
    fi
  fi
  echo "    to load the previous plist:" >&2
  echo "    $LAUNCHCTL bootstrap $LAUNCHD_DOMAIN $PLIST_BACKUP" >&2
  exit "$st"
}
trap '_recover_failed_reload' EXIT

# --- stage the release in a sibling dir, never in place ---------------------
# git archive reads the commit object, not the working tree, so an untracked
# or gitignored file on disk (credentials, scratch state, a stray .db) is
# invisible to it — this is what makes it safe where `cp -r`/`rsync` would
# not be (rule 7). The whole tracked tree is exported: nothing here curates
# a subset, so a file added to the repo later needs no installer change to
# reach the deploy area.
mkdir -p "$(dirname "$DEPLOY_DIR")"
STAGE="$DEPLOY_DIR.new"
rm -rf "$STAGE"
mkdir -p "$STAGE"

git -C "$REPO" archive "$SHA" | tar -x -C "$STAGE"
chmod +x "$STAGE/serve.sh"

# --- keep pip's own cache, and any source build's toolchain cache, out of
# $HOME --------------------------------------------------------------------
# pip writes its wheel/HTTP cache to $HOME/.cache by default on every
# invocation, whether or not anything ends up installed — including the
# internal pip run `python -m venv` shells out to via ensurepip to seed the
# new venv, before requirements.txt is ever read. A source build for a
# Rust-backed dependency (pydantic-core, tokenizers, py-rust-stemmers — none
# guaranteed a prebuilt wheel for every platform/interpreter combination)
# additionally invokes cargo, which creates $HOME/.cargo and $HOME/.rustup on
# first use unless CARGO_HOME/RUSTUP_HOME are already set. All three read
# $HOME from the environment, so all three would otherwise land inside
# whatever $HOME this script is running under — never acceptable (rule 3's
# principle, that nothing outside the deploy area and LaunchAgents gets
# written under $HOME, extends to this). Everything from here through the
# pip install below is confined to a scratch directory inside the staging
# area and removed once the install finishes; a failed install already
# discards the whole $STAGE, taking any partial cache with it. XDG_CACHE_HOME
# is set too as a catch-all for any other tool a future dependency might pull
# in that falls back to it.
BUILD_CACHE="$STAGE/.build-cache"
mkdir -p "$BUILD_CACHE"
export CARGO_HOME="$BUILD_CACHE/cargo"
export RUSTUP_HOME="$BUILD_CACHE/rustup"
export PIP_CACHE_DIR="$BUILD_CACHE/pip"
export XDG_CACHE_HOME="$BUILD_CACHE/xdg"

say "Building pinned interpreter ($PYTHON_BIN -m venv)"
"$PYTHON_BIN" -m venv "$STAGE/.venv"
"$STAGE/.venv/bin/pip" install --quiet --disable-pip-version-check \
    -r "$STAGE/requirements.txt"
rm -rf "$BUILD_CACHE"
unset CARGO_HOME RUSTUP_HOME PIP_CACHE_DIR XDG_CACHE_HOME

# --- rewrite the staged console scripts' shebangs for where they end up ----
# pip writes each console script (pip itself, and one per entry point any
# requirement declares — alembic, etc.) with an absolute shebang naming the
# interpreter AT THE PATH THE VENV STOOD WHEN PIP RAN: $STAGE/.venv/bin/
# python3.11. The swap below renames $STAGE to $DEPLOY_DIR, so every one of
# those shebangs is left naming a path that no longer exists (CQ-111).
# `python -m venv --upgrade` repairs neither: it refreshes the interpreter
# links and skips `setup_scripts` entirely, so it rewrites no activation
# script, and it leaves a console script pip already wrote untouched — both
# confirmed against a throwaway venv moved and then upgraded in place. That
# is why the activation-script branch below is load-bearing rather than a
# belt to `--upgrade`'s braces. So this rewrites
# the scripts themselves, by hand, here in the stage and before the swap:
# the swap stays the single atomic rename it already is, and every shebang
# is correct the moment it lands, with no window where $DEPLOY_DIR holds
# code whose scripts name a path that is about to vanish.
#
# Run with the staged venv's own python3 — never the shell — because the
# portable way to rewrite one line of a file without `sed -i`, whose flag
# syntax differs between BSD and GNU (platform/decisions/0010: this script
# runs on both), is the same small-inline-script shape _DB_READ_PY already
# uses above. Symlinks (python, python3, python3.11 itself) are left alone:
# pip never points a console script's shebang at one of those, and rewriting
# a symlink's own target is a different, uninvited change. The four
# activation scripts embed the same stage path as plain text, not a shebang
# — confirmed directly: activate, activate.csh and activate.fish each embed
# it as VIRTUAL_ENV=<path>, Activate.ps1 derives its venv directory at
# import time from its own script location and embeds no path at all — so
# the rewrite checks all four and only touches the three that carry it.
_REWRITE_VENV_SHEBANGS_PY='
import os
import sys

stage_venv, deploy_venv = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
bin_dir = os.path.join(stage_venv, "bin")
old_interp_prefix = "#!" + stage_venv + "/bin/"
activation_scripts = ("activate", "activate.csh", "activate.fish", "Activate.ps1")
rewritten = []

for name in sorted(os.listdir(bin_dir)):
    path = os.path.join(bin_dir, name)
    if os.path.islink(path) or not os.path.isfile(path):
        continue
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        data = f.read()
    nl = data.find(b"\n")
    first_line = data[:nl] if nl != -1 else data
    try:
        first_text = first_line.decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        continue
    if first_text.startswith(old_interp_prefix):
        new_first = "#!" + deploy_venv + "/bin/" + first_text[len(old_interp_prefix):]
        new_data = new_first.encode("utf-8") + data[len(first_line):]
        mode = os.stat(path).st_mode
        with open(path, "wb") as f:
            f.write(new_data)
        os.chmod(path, mode)
        rewritten.append(name)
    elif name in activation_scripts:
        try:
            text = data.decode("utf-8")
        except UnicodeDecodeError:
            continue
        if stage_venv in text:
            mode = os.stat(path).st_mode
            with open(path, "wb") as f:
                f.write(text.replace(stage_venv, deploy_venv).encode("utf-8"))
            os.chmod(path, mode)
            rewritten.append(name)

print(" ".join(rewritten))
'
say "Rewriting staged console-script shebangs for $DEPLOY_DIR"
"$STAGE/.venv/bin/python3" -c "$_REWRITE_VENV_SHEBANGS_PY" "$STAGE/.venv" "$DEPLOY_DIR/.venv" >/dev/null

{
  echo "ref: $REF"
  echo "commit: $SHA"
  echo "installed_at: $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
  echo "installed_from: $REPO"
} > "$STAGE/DEPLOYED_COMMIT"

# --- validate the staged build BEFORE it can become live --------------------
# serve.sh's own preflight is the interpreter-trust and import check this
# service has always started under (ownership walk, ACL check, `fastapi`/
# `uvicorn`/`sqlite_vec`/`fastembed` importability, and now `app` itself —
# see serve.sh's own "the app's own import-time configuration refusals" for
# why that last one was added) — reused here rather than re-implemented, so
# an install and a start are held to the same guard. `--dir` is not a
# serve.sh option: it derives DIR from its OWN resolved path, so running the
# STAGED copy is what makes this check about the staged build rather than
# the checkout. `SERVE_ALLOW_BRANCH` is unset here deliberately — $STAGE has
# no `.git` (git archive exports no repository), which is serve.sh's
# documented no-git passthrough, not a branch to bypass.
#
# `LIBRARY_CHECK_PLIST` names the plist whose `EnvironmentVariables` serve.sh
# imports `app` under — the one this install is ABOUT TO MAKE LIVE, never the
# live plist by default, because judging the new code against the environment
# it will actually run with is the whole point (library issue
# issue_01M3W62VVYJPQ6N9Y85DF3ZQYP: cd9693a's crash loop is exactly the gap a
# check against this installer's own shell environment would have missed
# again). When the plist write below is refused as drift, the candidate is
# never written and the OLD live plist is what stays loaded under the new
# code instead, so that is what gets checked.
CHECK_PLIST="$PLIST_CANDIDATE"
[ "$PLIST_REFUSED" -ne 1 ] || CHECK_PLIST="$PLIST_LIVE"
if ! LIBRARY_CHECK_PLIST="$CHECK_PLIST" "$STAGE/serve.sh" --check; then
  warn "the staged build failed serve.sh's own preflight — discarding it," \
       "the previous deploy (if any) is untouched"
  rm -rf "$STAGE"
  exit 1
fi

# --- swap: the deploy area is replaced as one unit, never merged with the
# previous release, so a partial file mix across two commits can't happen.
# Nothing above this line can have touched the live $DEPLOY_DIR. When this
# install is reloading the job, $OLD is kept rather than deleted here: a
# `launchctl bootstrap` failure below has to be able to restore the previous
# release tree, not just the previous plist (round 2 review, PR #157). It is
# removed once the reload verifies, or consumed by the recovery trap on a
# failure. When the plist isn't changing, nothing below can fail in a way
# that needs it, so it goes right away as before. Both moves are guarded
# explicitly: if the second one fails, this tries to undo the first on the
# spot — but that restoring `mv` can itself fail, and when it does, this
# does NOT claim the previous release was restored and does NOT clear $OLD
# (round 4 review, PR #157): it fails closed, naming $OLD's actual path so
# an operator can move it back by hand, and $DEPLOY_DIR IS left empty in
# that case. ---
OLD=""
if [ -d "$DEPLOY_DIR" ]; then
  OLD="$DEPLOY_DIR.old.$$"
  if ! mv "$DEPLOY_DIR" "$OLD"; then
    echo "install-library: could not move $DEPLOY_DIR aside for the swap — the previous release is untouched" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
fi
if [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_PRIMARY_SWAP:-0}" = "1" ] || ! mv "$STAGE" "$DEPLOY_DIR"; then
  if [ -n "$OLD" ]; then
    # INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_RECOVERY_SWAP also forces this restoring
    # `mv` to fail (see the trap's own use of the same seam further down —
    # it is the identical command, `mv "$OLD" "$DEPLOY_DIR"`).
    if [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_RECOVERY_SWAP:-0}" = "1" ] || ! mv "$OLD" "$DEPLOY_DIR" 2>/dev/null; then
      echo "install-library: could not move the staged build into $DEPLOY_DIR, and could not restore the previous release from $OLD — move it back by hand: mv $OLD $DEPLOY_DIR" >&2
    else
      OLD=""
      echo "install-library: could not move the staged build into $DEPLOY_DIR — the previous release was restored" >&2
    fi
  else
    echo "install-library: could not move the staged build into $DEPLOY_DIR" >&2
  fi
  exit 1
fi
SWAP_DONE=1
if [ "$RELOADING" -ne 1 ] && [ -n "$OLD" ]; then
  rm -rf "$OLD"
  OLD=""
fi

say "Deployed: $DEPLOY_DIR (commit $SHA)"

# --- verify: run one of the rewritten console scripts for real, rather than
# grepping its shebang, so a version string that only LOOKS right is not
# read as proof. Warns only (CQ-111): the service never execs a console
# script itself (serve.sh execs $DEPLOY_DIR/.venv/bin/python3 directly), the
# code has already swapped in, and a plist reload below may already be
# under way — undoing the install over a broken console script would be a
# bigger outage than the thing it's catching, for a path nothing serving
# traffic depends on.
PIP_VERSION_OUT=""
if ! PIP_VERSION_OUT="$("$DEPLOY_DIR/.venv/bin/pip" --version 2>&1)"; then
  warn "$DEPLOY_DIR/.venv/bin/pip --version failed after the swap: $PIP_VERSION_OUT"
else
  case "$PIP_VERSION_OUT" in
    *"$DEPLOY_DIR/.venv"*) ;;
    *) warn "$DEPLOY_DIR/.venv/bin/pip --version did not name $DEPLOY_DIR/.venv: $PIP_VERSION_OUT" ;;
  esac
fi

# --- plist: written now, after the code swap above, from the SAME candidate
# the decision at the top of this script already made (never re-read, never
# re-compared) — a drift refusal above already skipped this whole install's
# plist handling, and left $PLIST_CANDIDATE removed and $PLIST_LIVE untouched.
# Checked explicitly (round 4 review, PR #157): the code has already swapped
# in by this point, so leaving this `mv`'s own failure to fall through
# un-checked risked $PLIST_LIVE sitting stale — still the PREVIOUS plist's
# bytes — while $DEPLOY_DIR already holds the new release. Failing here
# still reaches the trap below the same as any other failure past this
# point (RELOADING was already decided at the top), which runs the ordinary
# pair-restore.
if [ "$PLIST_REFUSED" -ne 1 ]; then
  if [ "${INSTALL_LIBRARY_TEST_FAIL_PLIST_WRITE:-0}" = "1" ] || ! mv "$PLIST_CANDIDATE" "$PLIST_LIVE"; then
    echo "install-library: could not write $PLIST_LIVE from the staged candidate — $PLIST_CANDIDATE is kept" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  say "Plist installed: $PLIST_LIVE"
fi

if [ "$RELOADING" -ne 1 ]; then
  [ "$PLIST_REFUSED" -ne 1 ] && _write_plist_record "$PLIST_LIVE"
  say "Plist unchanged — no reload needed"
fi

# --- bootstrap: only reached when the top of this script already took the
# job down for exactly this reason. $DEPLOY_DIR holds $SHA's code and
# $PLIST_LIVE holds $SHA's plist by this point, so this brings both up
# together, from one commit, in the one restart the job spends here. --------
if [ "$RELOADING" -eq 1 ]; then
  say "Bootstrapping $LABEL with the new plist"
  if ! "$LAUNCHCTL" bootstrap "$LAUNCHD_DOMAIN" "$PLIST_LIVE"; then
    echo "install-library: $LAUNCHCTL bootstrap failed for $LABEL — the job is NOT loaded" >&2
    exit 1
  fi

  # --- verify: `state = running` alone is what ADR-0059's own --status
  # distinction (`we told it to` vs `it's true`) already demanded of a
  # bootstrap outcome, but a running process is not proof it is running
  # under the environment this bootstrap just supplied — only `launchctl
  # print`'s own `environment` block says that (security review, PR #157
  # round 1), and only an exact match against it, from the RIGHT block only
  # (round 3), says that reliably. _verify_reload does all of this, with
  # bounded retries, and is the same check the recovery trap runs against a
  # restored pair, not a second copy of it. ------------------------------
  if _verify_reload "$PLIST_LIVE"; then
    say "Reloaded: $LABEL is loaded, running, and the new environment is in effect"
    _write_plist_record "$PLIST_LIVE"
    rm -f "$PLIST_BACKUP"
    [ -n "$OLD" ] && rm -rf "$OLD"
    OLD=""
  else
    # Step 6 of the table above: bootstrap already returned 0, so
    # auto-recovery is deliberately not attempted here — set
    # PRESERVE_FOR_OPERATOR so the trap leaves $OLD and $PLIST_BACKUP
    # exactly as they are, and name both in the message below (never
    # deleted by the message that names them).
    PRESERVE_FOR_OPERATOR=1
    echo "install-library: $LABEL did not verify within ${VERIFY_TIMEOUT}s of reload:" >&2
    if [ "$PRINT_OK" -ne 1 ]; then
      echo "    $LAUNCHCTL print $LABEL failed:" >&2
      echo "$JOB_STATE" | sed 's/^/        /' >&2
    else
      case "$JOB_STATE" in
        *"state = running"*)
          if [ -n "$MISSING" ]; then
            echo "    running, but the live environment is missing:${MISSING}" >&2
          fi
          if [ "$DRAIN_DEADLINE_UNREADABLE" -eq 1 ]; then
            echo "    running, but could not read a top-level _DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS out of" >&2
            echo "    $DEPLOY_DIR/app.py" >&2
          fi
          if [ "$EXIT_TIMEOUT_UNREADABLE" -eq 1 ]; then
            echo "    running, but $LAUNCHCTL print $LAUNCHD_DOMAIN/$LABEL reported no 'exit timeout' line" >&2
          fi
          if [ "$EXIT_TIMEOUT_UNREADABLE" -eq 0 ] && [ "$DRAIN_DEADLINE_UNREADABLE" -eq 0 ] && \
             [ "$EXIT_TIMEOUT_INSUFFICIENT" -eq 1 ]; then
            echo "    running, but launchd's effective ExitTimeOut (${EXIT_TIMEOUT_ACTUAL}s) is not" >&2
            echo "    greater than app.py's _DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS (${DRAIN_DEADLINE}s), or the two" >&2
            echo "    could not be compared — a SIGTERM would be SIGKILLed before the graceful drain" >&2
            echo "    finishes" >&2
          fi
          ;;
        *)
          echo "    not running:" >&2
          echo "$JOB_STATE" | sed 's/^/        /' >&2 ;;
      esac
    fi
    if [ -n "$OLD" ] && [ -n "$PLIST_BACKUP" ]; then
      echo "  the previous release and plist are kept, not deleted, in case you roll back:" >&2
      echo "    previous release tree: $OLD" >&2
      echo "    previous plist:        $PLIST_BACKUP" >&2
      echo "  to roll back to the previous release:" >&2
      echo "    $LAUNCHCTL bootout $LAUNCHD_DOMAIN/$LABEL" >&2
      echo "    rm -rf $DEPLOY_DIR && mv $OLD $DEPLOY_DIR" >&2
      echo "    $LAUNCHCTL bootstrap $LAUNCHD_DOMAIN $PLIST_BACKUP" >&2
    fi
    echo "  to retry loading the new pair as installed:" >&2
    echo "    $LAUNCHCTL bootout $LAUNCHD_DOMAIN/$LABEL; $LAUNCHCTL bootstrap $LAUNCHD_DOMAIN $PLIST_LIVE" >&2
    echo "  to inspect: $LAUNCHCTL print $LAUNCHD_DOMAIN/$LABEL" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
fi

if [ "$RELOADING" -eq 1 ]; then
  cat <<EOF

Install complete. The plist changed, so $LABEL was taken down (bootout)
before this install staged anything, and reloaded (bootstrap) once the new
code and plist were both in place. Expect a gap in service across that
restart of roughly the drain time plus startup: up to app.py's
_DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS (${DRAIN_DEADLINE:-unknown}s) for the old process to quiesce,
plus however long the new one takes to come up. This install already
verified $LABEL came back up running the new environment before printing
this.

To confirm what it's actually serving, compare "commit" against
"staged_commit" in:
  curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8100/health

To check what commit is currently deployed:
  $0 --status
EOF
else
  # Read fresh here (never cached from the RELOADING=1 branch's own
  # _verify_reload call, which this branch never runs) so the drain bound
  # named below is the deployed app.py's own value, not a guess.
  DRAIN_DEADLINE="$(_drain_deadline_seconds "$DEPLOY_DIR/app.py")"
  cat <<EOF

Install complete. The plist did not change, so $LABEL was not reloaded —
this script neither loaded, unloaded, nor restarted it. The running
process restarts ITSELF onto this release with no operator step needed:
it polls its own deploy area for a staged commit that differs from what
it has loaded, and once it sees one, refuses new work, drains whatever is
already in flight, and exits so launchd's KeepAlive brings it back up on
the new code — a graceful restart, not the hard one \`launchctl bootout\`/
\`bootstrap\` would be, which is why this script does not hand you those
commands. Expect that restart within moments if the service was idle, or
up to app.py's own _DRAIN_DEADLINE_SECONDS (${DRAIN_DEADLINE:-unknown}s)
if a request was in flight when it noticed.

To confirm the running process has picked up this release, compare
"commit" against "staged_commit" in:
  curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8100/health
They read the same once the restart has happened; until then, "commit"
still names the release this process loaded before this install ran.

To check what commit is currently deployed (staged, not necessarily yet
running):
  $0 --status
EOF
fi
